WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.992 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAK] [RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:10.380 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.630 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Welcome back.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.620 align:middle line:84%
Hope everybody had a restful
break, spent lots of time

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.140 align:middle line:84%
doing syntax in
their spare time.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.810 align:middle line:90%
Let's do a little syntax now.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.743 align:middle line:90%
I don't have any announcements.

00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:23.410 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions
about the things

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.360 align:middle line:84%
that I'm not announcing,
things that people

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.000 align:middle line:84%
are, like, wondering
about, where we start?

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.500 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.292 align:middle line:84%
So I just wanted to
start with some review,

00:00:32.292 --> 00:00:34.174 align:middle line:84%
just to remind
everybody where we were.

00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:38.300 align:middle line:84%
We were drawing
trees like this one.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.430 align:middle line:84%
So here's a tree for "I
will tickle the child

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.470 align:middle line:90%
with the feather."

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.710 align:middle line:84%
It's a tree that has various
kinds of information in it

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.720 align:middle line:84%
about, well, various
things, including substrings

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.000 align:middle line:84%
that we think are
treated as constituents

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.260 align:middle line:84%
for various kinds of
syntactic phenomena.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.120 align:middle line:84%
So this tree is meant
to reflect the fact

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.840 align:middle line:84%
that this sentence,
first of all,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.140 align:middle line:84%
has a meaning in which
I'm going to use a feather

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.580 align:middle line:90%
to tickle a child.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.370 align:middle line:84%
That's the meaning that's
been diagrammed here.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:14.120 align:middle line:84%
And that if you perform
certain operations that

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.030 align:middle line:84%
make it clear that this is the
tree that you've got, like,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.910 align:middle line:84%
topicalizing "the child," moving
"the child" to the beginning

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.770 align:middle line:84%
of the sentence,
"The child I will

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.583 align:middle line:84%
tickle with the feather,"
that's the only-- in fact,

00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.000 align:middle line:84%
the only meaning
that you can have

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.110 align:middle line:84%
is the meaning that
goes with this tree.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.800 align:middle line:84%
So is this all sounds
vaguely familiar?

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Are there questions
about this tree?

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.030 align:middle line:84%
Is anybody looking at this
tree and thinking, wait,

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.400 align:middle line:90%
which class am I in again?

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.550 align:middle line:90%
What is going on?

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.202 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:01:39.202 --> 00:01:41.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk about this
tree in a little bit of detail

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:41.960 align:middle line:90%
today.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.460 align:middle line:84%
But I just want to
make sure there's

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.875 align:middle line:84%
nothing here that's shocking
or distressing anybody.

00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:48.470 align:middle line:90%


00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.200 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things
we've talked about

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.360 align:middle line:84%
is that some of the
properties of that tree

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.560 align:middle line:90%
follow from selection.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.080 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
idea that there's

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:02.880 align:middle line:84%
a special kind of relation
that can hold between heads,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.450 align:middle line:84%
so the smallest
things in the tree,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.970 align:middle line:84%
the things that just
have words under them,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.729 align:middle line:84%
things like the
verb, a relation that

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.400 align:middle line:84%
can hold between those heads
and other kinds of phrases

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.250 align:middle line:90%
in the sentence.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.810 align:middle line:84%
So we've said that the verb
"tickle" selects the object

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.010 align:middle line:90%
noun phrase, "the child."

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:23.570 align:middle line:84%
And what we mean by that is that
the relation between the verb

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.030 align:middle line:84%
"tickle" and the
object, "the child,"

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.790 align:middle line:84%
is special in that
not every verb can

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.580 align:middle line:90%
be followed by an object.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.440 align:middle line:90%
So you can tickle a child.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.567 align:middle line:90%
You can devour a child.

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:35.150 align:middle line:90%
You shouldn't.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.230 align:middle line:90%
But it's grammatical.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.230 align:middle line:90%
You can write a child.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:42.230 align:middle line:84%
But there are other verbs for
which you just can't do this.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.700 align:middle line:90%
You can't "thrive the child."

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.840 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't make any sense.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.918 align:middle line:84%
So there are some
verbs-- classic way

00:02:47.918 --> 00:02:50.210 align:middle line:84%
to say this is there are some
verbs that are transitive

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.280 align:middle line:84%
and others that
are intransitive.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.830 align:middle line:84%
So in order to know whether
you can have an object

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:58.100 align:middle line:84%
or not, whether there can be
a noun phrase in that position

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.372 align:middle line:84%
or not, the sister
of the verb, you've

00:03:00.372 --> 00:03:01.580 align:middle line:90%
got to know what the verb is.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.800 align:middle line:84%
The verb tells you whether it
needs to have an object or not.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.470 align:middle line:84%
So there's this
relation of selection

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.020 align:middle line:84%
that holds between the
verb and the object.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.630 align:middle line:84%
The idea is when
we look up words

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.760 align:middle line:84%
like "tickle" and
"devour" and "write"

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.670 align:middle line:84%
in our mental lexicon, we'll
see various things about them,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.700 align:middle line:84%
including how
they're pronounced,

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.920 align:middle line:84%
but also whether they
can have objects or not,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.160 align:middle line:84%
whether they select for
noun phrase sisters or not.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.400 align:middle line:84%
So when we ask, going
back to the tree,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.953 align:middle line:84%
why is the sister of the verb,
the noun phrase "the child,"

00:03:34.953 --> 00:03:37.370 align:middle line:84%
one way of interpreting that
question, the answer is well,

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.100 align:middle line:84%
it's because the verb "tickle"
selects for a noun phrase

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.180 align:middle line:90%
sister.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.940 align:middle line:90%
And so you give it one.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's why the tree
has that bit of structure.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.230 align:middle line:84%
And we can tell
that kind of story

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.920 align:middle line:84%
about a lot of
parts of this tree.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.380 align:middle line:84%
So similarly,
we're going to want

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.600 align:middle line:84%
to say that the
preposition "with" selects

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.972 align:middle line:84%
for a sister that's a noun
phrase, "the feather."

00:03:56.972 --> 00:03:58.430 align:middle line:84%
And for that matter,
we'll probably

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.860 align:middle line:84%
want to say that the
T, "will," is selecting

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.170 align:middle line:84%
for a verb phrase that's
why the T has a verb phrase,

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.463 align:middle line:90%
it's its sister.

00:04:04.463 --> 00:04:06.380 align:middle line:84%
So there are various
places in this tree where

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.300 align:middle line:90%
a head has a particular sister.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And it's because the
head is selecting

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.180 align:middle line:90%
for that as its sister.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:16.540 align:middle line:90%


00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.160 align:middle line:84%
We also said that this relation
of selection that is something

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.410 align:middle line:84%
that heads can do-- they
can select for properties

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.970 align:middle line:90%
of their sister--

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.160 align:middle line:84%
that sometimes, at least,
they select more specifically

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:28.660 align:middle line:90%
than that.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:33.070 align:middle line:84%
They select for sisters
where the sister's head

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.520 align:middle line:90%
has a particular property.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.240 align:middle line:84%
So I think the example
I gave you last time was

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.070 align:middle line:90%
the verb depends--

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.860 align:middle line:84%
where the verb depends needs
a prepositional phrase sister,

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.860 align:middle line:84%
but it specifically needs
a prepositional phrase

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.230 align:middle line:84%
sister in which the
preposition is "on."

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:48.880 align:middle line:90%
So you have to depend on things.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.220 align:middle line:84%
You can't depend from
them or depend at them.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.050 align:middle line:84%
You can't just have
any preposition there.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.850 align:middle line:90%
It has to be "on."

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.030 align:middle line:84%
So there are verbs
like "depend--"

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.980 align:middle line:84%
and there are many
verbs like this--

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.643 align:middle line:84%
that select for a particular
prepositional phrase-- sisters,

00:05:02.643 --> 00:05:04.810 align:middle line:84%
but in particular, prepositional
phrase sisters that

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.540 align:middle line:90%
have a particular preposition.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.590 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it's less
particular than that.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:15.910 align:middle line:90%
So I think in class I said--

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.460 align:middle line:84%
(he said, vamping until he
could get some chalk out)--

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.540 align:middle line:84%
that the verb "put"
seems to select

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.270 align:middle line:84%
for a noun phrase sister and
prepositional phrase sister.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:36.970 align:middle line:84%
So you put the book "on the
table" or "in the refrigerator"

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:41.320 align:middle line:84%
or "under the car"
or whatever, wherever

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.280 align:middle line:90%
it is you want to put the book.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.763 align:middle line:84%
So the "put" has to have
a noun phrase object,

00:05:45.763 --> 00:05:46.930 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have to be the book.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.013 align:middle line:90%
It can be any noun phrase.

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:51.040 align:middle line:84%
And then there can be
a prepositional phrase.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.222 align:middle line:90%
"Put" is not like "depend."

00:05:52.222 --> 00:05:54.430 align:middle line:84%
There are a variety of
prepositions that can be here.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.305 align:middle line:84%
But you can't just have
any preposition here.

00:05:56.305 --> 00:05:58.900 align:middle line:84%
So you can't say "Put the
book during the party."

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.320 align:middle line:90%


00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.700 align:middle line:90%
That's not a possible sentence.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:09.640 align:middle line:84%
And we might hope to
derive that from properties

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.080 align:middle line:90%
of the meaning of "put," right?

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.360 align:middle line:84%
So the prepositional
phrase that goes with "put"

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.760 align:middle line:84%
needs to specify
a location, right?

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.420 align:middle line:84%
And "During the party" doesn't
specify a location unless we're

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.540 align:middle line:84%
thinking of time as space,
which as long as syntax

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:25.420 align:middle line:84%
is not Doctor Who,
I think we're OK.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:25.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.737 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So for clarity,
because Merge only is binary--

00:06:29.737 --> 00:06:30.820 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, dear.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:31.320 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.340 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.810 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.250 align:middle line:90%
So Merge is only binary.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.590 align:middle line:84%
And that means if
we're going to have

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.510 align:middle line:84%
a tree for these
kinds of sentences,

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:43.960 align:middle line:84%
we're going to need
to put the verb "put."

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.970 align:middle line:84%
And since we want
the noun phrase

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.490 align:middle line:84%
to be next-- that's what
this seems to tell us--

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:49.840 align:middle line:90%
we'll have a noun phrase here.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.390 align:middle line:90%


00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:59.037 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll have a
prepositional phrase

00:06:59.037 --> 00:07:00.870 align:middle line:84%
That's one possible
answer to your question.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.203 align:middle line:90%
If Merge is going to be binary--

00:07:02.203 --> 00:07:06.648 align:middle line:90%


00:07:06.648 --> 00:07:08.190 align:middle line:84%
if Merge is going
to be binary, we're

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.710 align:middle line:84%
going to want to
say, yeah, "put"

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.582 align:middle line:84%
is something that
needs two things.

00:07:13.582 --> 00:07:15.790 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have to give
them to it one at a time.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:17.410 align:middle line:84%
We can't give them
both to it at once.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.070 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, if we
wanted to do that,

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:20.820 align:middle line:84%
we would need to use
ternary Merge, right?

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.660 align:middle line:84%
We'd need to merge
three things at once.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.270 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll merge
them one at a time.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.770 align:middle line:84%
We'll first give
it the noun phrase.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.300 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll give it
the prepositional phrase.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.220 align:middle line:84%
Why do we first give it
the noun phrase and then

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:30.262 align:middle line:90%
the prepositional phrase?

00:07:30.262 --> 00:07:33.000 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we say, "Put
on the table the book"?

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.530 align:middle line:90%
We might actually get to that.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.850 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going
to get to it today.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.150 align:middle line:84%
So this is one possible
answer to your question.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.960 align:middle line:84%
Another possible answer to
your question, of course,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.510 align:middle line:84%
would be to say,
ah, we've discovered

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.120 align:middle line:84%
that we were wrong to say that
there's binary Merge, right?

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.288 align:middle line:84%
That here's a place where we
need there to be ternary merge.

00:07:48.288 --> 00:07:49.830 align:middle line:84%
This is going to
interact with things

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:51.820 align:middle line:84%
we're going to want to say
about how selection works.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.320 align:middle line:84%
So I have now a
couple of times said

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.160 align:middle line:84%
a thing gets to select for
the properties of its sister,

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:56.970 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.310 align:middle line:84%
And here it's selecting for
properties of its sister.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:00.990 align:middle line:84%
But this is not
its sister, right?

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.760 align:middle line:90%
This is something else.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.800 align:middle line:90%
It's-- never mind.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.200 align:middle line:84%
I can't figure out the family
relation between these two

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.040 align:middle line:90%
points on the tree.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.380 align:middle line:90%
It's-- what is it?

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:12.628 align:middle line:90%
It's its aunt, yeah.

00:08:12.628 --> 00:08:14.670 align:middle line:84%
The prepositional phrase
is the aunt of the verb.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.850 align:middle line:84%
That's not how
actual syntacticians

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:18.100 align:middle line:90%
talk about this kind of thing.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.550 align:middle line:90%
So they're not sisters.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.320 align:middle line:84%
So two kinds of
things we can say.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.650 align:middle line:84%
Oh, what we're discovering is
that we need ternary Merge.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.750 align:middle line:84%
We need these to
both be sisters.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.180 align:middle line:84%
Another would be
to say, oh, we're

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.789 align:middle line:84%
discovering that selection
isn't necessarily

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.039 align:middle line:90%
for properties of your sister.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:38.350 align:middle line:84%
It's something more
like you select things.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.418 align:middle line:84%
The first things that you merge
must be things that you select.

00:08:41.418 --> 00:08:43.960 align:middle line:84%
We're going to circle around
and talk about that in a second.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.905 align:middle line:84%
So to say something more
elaborate about how you select.

00:08:46.905 --> 00:08:48.280 align:middle line:84%
So that's a really
good question.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.740 align:middle line:84%
What we really need
to do is develop

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.240 align:middle line:84%
tests that will
tell us whether this

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.340 align:middle line:84%
is the right tree for
something like this,

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.910 align:middle line:84%
or whether we would rather
have something like this, where

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.910 align:middle line:84%
there's a verb and a noun
phrase and prepositional phrase,

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.540 align:middle line:84%
and Merge doesn't
have to be binary.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.290 align:middle line:84%
Just to cheat a little
bit, what we would find out

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.790 align:middle line:84%
when we develop
these kinds of tests

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.873 align:middle line:84%
is that this is not the
kind of tree that we want.

00:09:10.873 --> 00:09:12.510 align:middle line:84%
We want trees that
are like this.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.520 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to
have to do some work

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.810 align:middle line:90%
to develop those tests.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:17.310 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have
a scary thought.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.112 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, no.

00:09:20.112 --> 00:09:22.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The thought is
like-- so about selected--

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:23.100 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:24.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like
there are some verbs,

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.157 align:middle line:90%
like, you say "board."

00:09:25.157 --> 00:09:26.100 align:middle line:90%
Like you board a ship.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.403 align:middle line:84%
You kind of have, like, a
secret selection inside of it

00:09:28.403 --> 00:09:29.820 align:middle line:84%
because you could
think of "board"

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.005 align:middle line:90%
as like get on the ship.

00:09:31.005 --> 00:09:32.130 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.838 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so you'd
be like, ah, "get"

00:09:33.838 --> 00:09:36.020 align:middle line:84%
is selecting for on the
ship, a certain type

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:37.020 align:middle line:90%
of prepositional phrase.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:37.260 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Mm-hmm.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or you could have just
"board," which kind of includes

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:40.920 align:middle line:90%
it inside of it.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.500 align:middle line:84%
Like, also in Spanish, if you
have words like "buscar," which

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.050 align:middle line:84%
is, like, "to look
for," and so it's, like,

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:50.490 align:middle line:84%
could you think of "put"
as a verb that maybe--

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.120 align:middle line:84%
like if English
had been different,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.308 align:middle line:84%
you could have a verb like
"You put the refrigerator."

00:09:55.308 --> 00:09:57.600 align:middle line:84%
Everyone knows that you mean
"put in the refrigerator."

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:58.650 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:09:59.483 align:middle line:90%
I see what you mean.

00:09:59.483 --> 00:10:04.620 align:middle line:84%
So you're pointing out something
important, which is that--

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.030 align:middle line:90%
here, let's do "look for."

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:07.890 align:middle line:84%
But in English,
we're going to want

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.320 align:middle line:84%
to have a verb
"look" that selects

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.455 align:middle line:84%
for a prepositional phrase,
where the preposition is "for."

00:10:13.455 --> 00:10:16.320 align:middle line:90%


00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:18.420 align:middle line:84%
But that there are other
languages like Spanish

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.628 align:middle line:84%
in which there's a single
verb that means "look for."

00:10:20.628 --> 00:10:23.730 align:middle line:84%
For that matter, in English,
there's a verb "seek."

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:25.350 align:middle line:90%
So it's kind of dramatic.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.717 align:middle line:84%
But if I go into a bookstore and
say, "I'm looking for a book,"

00:10:28.717 --> 00:10:30.300 align:middle line:84%
that's the normal
thing for me to say.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:33.970 align:middle line:84%
I could also go into a bookstore
and say "I am seeking a book."

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.990 align:middle line:84%
They would probably direct me
to the fantasy, science fiction

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.420 align:middle line:90%
section if I did that.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:43.170 align:middle line:84%
But those are both two
ways to say the same thing.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the way to think
about this is, yeah, Spanish

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.330 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have a verb
that means "look for."

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.100 align:middle line:90%
It has a verb that means "seek."

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:54.060 align:middle line:90%
And-- there.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:54.780 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.197 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe another
way to say this--

00:10:56.197 --> 00:10:58.560 align:middle line:84%
and this is going to be
important as you guys are doing

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.085 align:middle line:84%
your work with the languages
that you're working on--

00:11:01.085 --> 00:11:02.460 align:middle line:84%
is that you
shouldn't necessarily

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.250 align:middle line:84%
expect the selectional
properties of a verb

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.280 align:middle line:84%
to be the same from one
language to the next.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.205 align:middle line:84%
That if you are inclined
to think of Spanish--

00:11:11.205 --> 00:11:13.140 align:middle line:90%
is it "buscar"?--

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.570 align:middle line:84%
as the equivalent of
"look for," that we

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.660 align:middle line:84%
want to think of
that as-- as-- that

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.655 align:middle line:84%
English has a verb
"look" that selects

00:11:20.655 --> 00:11:21.780 align:middle line:90%
for a prepositional phrase.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.090 align:middle line:84%
Spanish has a verb "buscar"
that's like "seek."

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:27.070 align:middle line:84%
It selects for a noun
phrase complement.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:27.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.370 align:middle line:84%
So you want to be
careful as you are going

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:31.170 align:middle line:90%
from language to language.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.920 align:middle line:84%
You can't trust
selectional restrictions.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:38.700 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, so I've used
"put" as the example

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:42.690 align:middle line:84%
of a verb that has two things
that it seems to select.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.700 align:middle line:84%
But there are probably
examples of verbs

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.690 align:middle line:84%
that select two things, where
the two things are, say,

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.930 align:middle line:90%
both noun phrases.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:57.450 align:middle line:84%
So take a verb like "give
the children books."

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:01.500 align:middle line:84%
So here we want there to be two
noun phrases coming together

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.070 align:middle line:90%
with "give."

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:04.110 align:middle line:90%
And "give" selects those.

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.960 align:middle line:84%
It's a property of
"give" that it can

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.490 align:middle line:90%
have two noun phrases after it.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:09.900 align:middle line:90%
And not every verb can do that.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.840 align:middle line:90%
"Put," for example, can't.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.900 align:middle line:84%
So even verbs that are fairly
similar in their meaning,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:16.980 align:middle line:90%
like "donate"--

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:18.750 align:middle line:84%
so you can't "donate
the children books."

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.780 align:middle line:84%
You have to donate
books to the children.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:25.080 align:middle line:84%
So yes, as so often
in this class,

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.690 align:middle line:84%
I'm showing you the
easiest examples here.

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.580 align:middle line:84%
And as so often in
this class, you guys

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:30.870 align:middle line:84%
are immediately saying,
wait, wait, what

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.162 align:middle line:90%
about the complicated examples?

00:12:32.162 --> 00:12:34.200 align:middle line:84%
So, yes, there are more
complicated examples.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:35.490 align:middle line:84%
We'll try to get to
them, cases where you're

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:36.740 align:middle line:90%
selecting more than one thing.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:38.072 align:middle line:90%
We'll come back to that.

00:12:38.072 --> 00:12:39.280 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about it more now.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:39.780 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.973 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So for give
the children books,

00:12:41.973 --> 00:12:43.140 align:middle line:90%
how would you continue that?

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.670 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.940 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So if
we use the technology

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:51.240 align:middle line:84%
that we have right now,
we're kind of compelled

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.890 align:middle line:90%
to have a tree like this one.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:13:02.610 align:middle line:90%


00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.350 align:middle line:84%
I wrote this as
capital N because I

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.690 align:middle line:84%
knew I was about to
write a noun phrase.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:08.220 align:middle line:90%
"Give the children books."

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:12.660 align:middle line:84%
So where "give" needs to-- to
combine with two noun phrases.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:13.770 align:middle line:90%
And Merge is binary.

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:16.490 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll do
binary merge twice.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:18.990 align:middle line:84%
That's a kind of tree that we
could draw with the techniques

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.630 align:middle line:90%
that we have right now.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:29.230 align:middle line:84%
I hope I hedged enough in there
to make you feel emotionally

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:33.160 align:middle line:84%
grounded enough that if it
turns out later that that's not

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.580 align:middle line:84%
the right tree, that you
won't be too disappointed

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.830 align:middle line:84%
because we're going to
talk more about this stuff.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:41.432 align:middle line:84%
And our understanding will
get more sophisticated.

00:13:41.432 --> 00:13:43.390 align:middle line:84%
But given everything I'm
telling you right now,

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:45.890 align:middle line:90%
that's the tree we expect.

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:47.690 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:51.775 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do these trees have
to have the words in sentence?

00:13:51.775 --> 00:13:52.900 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.480 align:middle line:90%
I missed the middle of that.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:58.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do these
trees have to have

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.880 align:middle line:84%
exactly each and every
word in each sentence?

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:05.590 align:middle line:90%
Or could it fill in some words?

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.870 align:middle line:84%
For example, I learned
in elementary school

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.180 align:middle line:84%
the subject of every
command is "you."

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.485 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, mm-hmm.

00:14:11.485 --> 00:14:15.976 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could you have
"you" in parentheses

00:14:15.976 --> 00:14:17.085 align:middle line:90%
in one of these trees?

00:14:17.085 --> 00:14:18.210 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:19.918 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "Give (to)
the children books."

00:14:19.918 --> 00:14:20.970 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:21.810 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.790 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.220 align:middle line:84%
That's a really interesting
and difficult question.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.790 align:middle line:90%
The short answer is yes.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.740 align:middle line:84%
There absolutely
are places where

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.930 align:middle line:84%
we're going to want to say I'm
showing you trees in which I'm

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.180 align:middle line:90%
not showing you trees anymore.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.710 align:middle line:90%
I'm showing you a nebula.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.440 align:middle line:90%
Give me just a second.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:45.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm showing you trees in
which every terminal node,

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:49.350 align:middle line:84%
every head has
one word under it.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:51.750 align:middle line:84%
And you're wondering,
could there

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.930 align:middle line:84%
be trees where
there are nodes that

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.060 align:middle line:84%
don't have anything
under them or don't have

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.570 align:middle line:90%
anything pronounced under them?

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:05.070 align:middle line:84%
And indeed the short
answer is yes, there surely

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.780 align:middle line:90%
are going to be such trees.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:10.320 align:middle line:90%
Your example is a good one.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:15.730 align:middle line:84%
Do I want to go off
and talk about that?

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.910 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, let's talk about
that for just a second.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:24.510 align:middle line:90%


00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.100 align:middle line:90%
We talked a little bit before--

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.080 align:middle line:84%
we're going to come
back to this later.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.680 align:middle line:84%
But we talked a little
bit before about--

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.100 align:middle line:90%
yeah, we can do this fast--

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:39.750 align:middle line:84%
constraints on things
that pronouns and names

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.380 align:middle line:90%
can refer to.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:44.400 align:middle line:84%
So for example, we talked
about the fact that if you say,

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:49.800 align:middle line:84%
"She likes Sally," that's
a perfectly fine sentence.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:55.360 align:middle line:84%
But it can't mean that she
and Sally are the same person,

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:55.860 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.590 align:middle line:84%
So "she" has to refer to
somebody other than Sally.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:02.828 align:middle line:84%
And we developed a rule
that has that consequence.

00:16:02.828 --> 00:16:05.370 align:middle line:84%
And I promised you that we would
talk about that rule in more

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.390 align:middle line:90%
depth later on.

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:10.660 align:middle line:90%
Here's another kind of case.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:20.040 align:middle line:84%
If you say something like
"Sally defended herself,"

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.100 align:middle line:84%
there's this special
expression "herself."

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.950 align:middle line:84%
And it has to refer
to Sally, right?

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:29.490 align:middle line:84%
So "Sally defended herself"
means Sally defended Sally.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:33.420 align:middle line:84%
So "herself" refers back
to whoever the subject is.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:35.610 align:middle line:84%
If this had been "Mary
defended herself," well,

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.500 align:middle line:84%
it would have to be
Mary defending Mary.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:44.970 align:middle line:84%
If it had been, "I
defended myself," well,

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:48.000 align:middle line:84%
then you have to use this
special form of these words,

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:49.290 align:middle line:90%
these "-self" words.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:50.400 align:middle line:90%
They're called reflexives.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:55.950 align:middle line:90%


00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.110 align:middle line:84%
These reflexives
have to refer back

00:16:58.110 --> 00:16:59.670 align:middle line:84%
to something else
in the sentence.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.867 align:middle line:84%
In these examples, they
always refer to the subject.

00:17:02.867 --> 00:17:04.200 align:middle line:90%
And they have to do that, right?

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:15.280 align:middle line:84%
So you can't say things like, "I
defended herself" or "himself,"

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:15.780 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.069 align:middle line:90%
These are out, yeah?

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:20.490 align:middle line:84%
So the reflexive that's at
the end of these sentences

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:23.250 align:middle line:84%
has to refer back to the
subject in these cases.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:25.230 align:middle line:84%
And the form of the
reflexive tells you

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:26.730 align:middle line:84%
something about
what the subject is.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.640 align:middle line:90%
So you get, it's got--

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:30.540 align:middle line:90%
so-- hmm.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.100 align:middle line:84%
You get "herself"
when the subject

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.870 align:middle line:90%
is feminine and third person.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:36.870 align:middle line:84%
And you got "myself" when
the subject is first person.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:42.750 align:middle line:84%
And if it were "you
defended," it would

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.570 align:middle line:90%
be "defended yourself," yeah?

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.530 align:middle line:84%
So this reflexive is
telling you that it's

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:48.780 align:middle line:90%
referring back to "you," yeah?

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.700 align:middle line:84%
This means you
defended you, yeah.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.367 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you all this
because your example

00:17:53.367 --> 00:17:55.200 align:middle line:84%
from elementary school
is a really good one.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:18:02.450 align:middle line:84%
If I tell you, defend
yourself, it's a command.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, everything that we've now
learned about these reflexes

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:08.010 align:middle line:84%
leads us to hope that there's
a "you" in that sentence

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:10.710 align:middle line:84%
because, well, that's what
reflexives seemed to do.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.020 align:middle line:90%
They refer back to the subject.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.017 align:middle line:84%
And they have a form
that tells you something

00:18:16.017 --> 00:18:17.100 align:middle line:90%
about what the subject is.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.690 align:middle line:84%
In this case, they tell
you that the subject

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:21.240 align:middle line:84%
is "you," which is what you were
taught in elementary school,

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:22.330 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:23.970 align:middle line:90%
So you say "Defend yourself!"

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:24.810 align:middle line:90%
And that's fine.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:31.720 align:middle line:84%
And you cannot say "Defend
himself!" or "Defend myself!"--

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:32.220 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.080 align:middle line:84%
Just as you cannot
say you defended--

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.620 align:middle line:84%
you can say you
defended yourself,

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:41.430 align:middle line:84%
but not "You defended myself"
or "himself" or "herself," yeah?

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:43.080 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, there are
some good reasons

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:44.872 align:middle line:84%
to think that whoever
told you that there's

00:18:44.872 --> 00:18:47.010 align:middle line:84%
a "you" in this
sentence was right.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.560 align:middle line:84%
You can detect the "you" by
using these reflexives, yeah.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:54.240 align:middle line:84%
So there are, indeed, words
that are not pronounced.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.740 align:middle line:84%
And if we were going to draw
a tree for defend yourself,

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:58.407 align:middle line:84%
we'd probably want
to say, yeah, there's

00:18:58.407 --> 00:18:59.647 align:middle line:90%
a "you" in subject position.

00:18:59.647 --> 00:19:01.980 align:middle line:84%
And there would have to be a
rule saying don't pronounce

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:02.897 align:middle line:90%
that particular "you."

00:19:02.897 --> 00:19:05.480 align:middle line:84%
So the short answer
to your question

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:08.060 align:middle line:84%
is, yes, there are parts of
trees that are not pronounced.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:12.410 align:middle line:84%
And we may get a chance
to talk more about.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:12.920 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:14.270 align:middle line:90%
That was a tangent.

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:15.950 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:17.750 align:middle line:84%
Take us back to
this main line here.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:21.948 align:middle line:90%


00:19:21.948 --> 00:19:22.490 align:middle line:90%
Where are we?

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so selection.

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.120 align:middle line:84%
Heads select for properties
of their sisters,

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.740 align:middle line:84%
although several
of you want to know

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:29.570 align:middle line:84%
what happens when they seem
to have more than one thing

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:30.470 align:middle line:90%
that they select.

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:33.050 align:middle line:90%
And I'm trying to--

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:35.750 align:middle line:84%
well, I'm not exactly ruthlessly
suppressing that question.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:37.430 align:middle line:84%
But I'm telling you
what we currently

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.460 align:middle line:84%
seem to have to say about
it and maybe flagging

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.970 align:middle line:84%
the possibility that we'll have
to say different things later.

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.660 align:middle line:90%
So far so good?

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.910 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah, right.

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:51.520 align:middle line:84%
And when selection is
for something specific--

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:54.640 align:middle line:84%
so this was the point I was
trying to make with this slide.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.250 align:middle line:84%
When a verb selects
for something specific,

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.040 align:middle line:84%
a verb can, for example, say
I need a prepositional phrase.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.620 align:middle line:84%
And I need the
preposition to be "on."

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:04.348 align:middle line:84%
Or I need a
prepositional phrase--

00:20:04.348 --> 00:20:05.140 align:middle line:90%
this is like "put."

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:06.310 align:middle line:90%
I need a prepositional phrase.

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:08.477 align:middle line:84%
And I need the preposition
to be something locative.

00:20:08.477 --> 00:20:11.500 align:middle line:84%
It needs to describe
a location, yeah.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.780 align:middle line:84%
So "depend" selects for
a prepositional phrase

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:15.770 align:middle line:90%
with the head "on."

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:18.490 align:middle line:84%
We're never going to find, for
example, a verb that selects

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:19.690 align:middle line:90%
for a prepositional phrase--

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:21.398 align:middle line:84%
and I don't care what
the preposition is,

00:20:21.398 --> 00:20:23.085 align:middle line:84%
but the object
must be "tomatoes."

00:20:23.085 --> 00:20:24.460 align:middle line:84%
We won't find
anything like that.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:28.360 align:middle line:84%
So verbs select
for their sisters--

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.120 align:middle line:84%
sisters in all the cases
I'm going to show you.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:32.650 align:middle line:90%
They select for their sisters.

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:34.570 align:middle line:84%
But what they
select specifically

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:36.610 align:middle line:84%
is sisters with a
particular head.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:38.440 align:middle line:84%
They don't say
things like, I select

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:41.380 align:middle line:84%
for a sister whose
complement must be this

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:44.140 align:middle line:84%
or which must contain
a tomato or which

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:45.837 align:middle line:90%
must be modified by an adverb.

00:20:45.837 --> 00:20:47.170 align:middle line:90%
Heads don't do things like that.

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:49.150 align:middle line:84%
They select for properties
of their sisters,

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:52.025 align:middle line:84%
specifically the heads
of their sisters.

00:20:52.025 --> 00:20:54.400 align:middle line:84%
And once we know that-- this
is what I said in class last

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:55.300 align:middle line:90%
time--

00:20:55.300 --> 00:21:00.460 align:middle line:84%
we can use that
fact about selection

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:05.620 align:middle line:84%
to detect other heads,
so other kinds of things

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:08.440 align:middle line:84%
that seem to be in
this selection relation

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:11.200 align:middle line:84%
with other words in the
sentence, other heads

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:11.960 align:middle line:90%
in the sentence.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.890 align:middle line:84%
So when we see other
cases where there's

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:18.518 align:middle line:84%
a particular word in the
sentence whose value seems

00:21:18.518 --> 00:21:20.560 align:middle line:84%
to be determined by another
word in the sentence,

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:23.050 align:middle line:84%
we get to think, oh, OK, maybe
that's a selection relation.

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:25.810 align:middle line:84%
This was the example
I gave you last time.

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:30.280 align:middle line:84%
Verbs seem to be able
to select for properties

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:33.010 align:middle line:90%
of the clause that follows them.

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:34.990 align:middle line:84%
So there are verbs
like "think" that

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:36.580 align:middle line:90%
can be followed by a clause.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.980 align:middle line:84%
Not every verb can be
followed by a clause, right?

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:41.530 align:middle line:90%
So "I devoured the pizza."

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.570 align:middle line:84%
"Devour" can be followed
by a noun phrase.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.550 align:middle line:84%
But "I devoured that I
have won the lottery"

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:47.570 align:middle line:90%
doesn't make sense.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:50.780 align:middle line:84%
So this looks like a
selection relation.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.260 align:middle line:84%
And specifically, it's
a selection relation

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:56.980 align:middle line:84%
that's picky about these words
that introduce the clause that

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:59.440 align:middle line:84%
come at the beginning
of the clause, words

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:00.670 align:middle line:90%
like "that" and "whether."

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:04.240 align:middle line:84%
So "think" can be
followed by a clause where

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.340 align:middle line:84%
the word that introduces
the clauses is "that."

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:07.715 align:middle line:84%
You can say things
like, "I think

00:22:07.715 --> 00:22:09.070 align:middle line:90%
that I have won the lottery."

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:11.605 align:middle line:84%
"Wonder" can be followed
by a clause where

00:22:11.605 --> 00:22:13.480 align:middle line:84%
the word that introduces
the following clause

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.310 align:middle line:84%
is "whether," as in,
"I wonder whether I

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:16.480 align:middle line:90%
have won the lottery."

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:19.660 align:middle line:84%
And these verbs
can't be switched.

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:21.920 align:middle line:84%
So you can say "I think that
I have won the lottery."

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.620 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say "I think whether
I have won the lottery."

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.870 align:middle line:84%
You can say "I wonder whether
I have won the lottery."

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:29.380 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say "I wonder
that I have won the lottery."

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:32.132 align:middle line:84%
And I was saying this looks
kind of like "depend" and "on".

00:22:32.132 --> 00:22:34.090 align:middle line:84%
"Think" needs a clause
that starts with "that."

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.215 align:middle line:84%
"Wonder" needs a clause
that starts with "whether."

00:22:36.215 --> 00:22:38.650 align:middle line:84%
"Depend" needs a prepositional
phrase that has "on" in it.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.380 align:middle line:84%
And just like with "depend" and
"on," we said, yeah, "depend"

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:45.250 align:middle line:84%
is selecting for a prepositional
phrase with a particular head.

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:46.810 align:middle line:84%
We get to say,
oh, OK, apparently

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:48.520 align:middle line:84%
"that" and "whether"
are the heads

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:51.290 align:middle line:84%
of the clause that's getting
selected by the verb.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:53.350 align:middle line:84%
So "think" and
"wonder" are selecting

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:55.390 align:middle line:84%
for clauses that
are headed by words

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:56.500 align:middle line:90%
like "that" and "whether."

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:59.050 align:middle line:84%
We have a word for words
like "that" and "whether"--

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:03.010 align:middle line:90%
we call them "complementizers."

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:05.210 align:middle line:84%
I think I apologized
for that word last time.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.460 align:middle line:84%
I'll just apologize for it
again because it's probably

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:09.310 align:middle line:84%
not the word you
learned for those things

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:11.170 align:middle line:84%
in elementary school
or high school.

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:14.335 align:middle line:84%
You might have heard
them called conjunctions,

00:23:14.335 --> 00:23:16.810 align:middle line:84%
so "subordinating conjunctions,"
lots of things people

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:18.100 align:middle line:90%
call them.

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:20.290 align:middle line:84%
But in linguistics, we
call them complementizers.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:23.590 align:middle line:84%
So I'll have to ask you to
join me in calling them that.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:26.800 align:middle line:84%
So the abbreviation for
complementizer is C.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:31.780 align:middle line:84%
And the phrase is the sister of
verbs like "think" and "wonder"

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:34.240 align:middle line:84%
is a complementizer
phrase, a CP.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.550 align:middle line:84%
It's a phrase that's headed
by "that" or "whether,"

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:39.640 align:middle line:90%
these complementizers.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:40.708 align:middle line:90%
So I'll review.

00:23:40.708 --> 00:23:42.625 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully it sounds at
least vaguely familiar.

00:23:42.625 --> 00:23:46.360 align:middle line:90%


00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:48.738 align:middle line:84%
So now here we are back
at the original tree.

00:23:48.738 --> 00:23:50.530 align:middle line:84%
"I will tickle the
child with the feather."

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.270 align:middle line:84%
We've talked a lot about
selection relations.

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:54.312 align:middle line:84%
We've said that selection
relations are relations

00:23:54.312 --> 00:24:00.160 align:middle line:84%
between heads and phrases, many
cases, the sister of the head.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.770 align:middle line:84%
And in particular,
selection relations

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:04.960 align:middle line:84%
seem to be picky
about when there

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:08.890 align:middle line:84%
is pickiness about the
head of the phrase that's

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:09.648 align:middle line:90%
getting selected.

00:24:09.648 --> 00:24:12.190 align:middle line:84%
So we've seen examples of that
now with prepositional phrases

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:17.980 align:middle line:84%
and now with Complementizer
Phrases, CPs, not clauses.

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:22.370 align:middle line:84%
But-- and this is near the
end of what we did last time--

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:26.230 align:middle line:84%
there is more to
life than selection,

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:27.850 align:middle line:84%
not much more,
but a little more.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:34.970 align:middle line:84%
So we don't want to say
that "tickle" or "child" is

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:37.550 align:middle line:84%
selecting "with a
feather," right?

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:42.890 align:middle line:84%
So when I told you that
tickle is selecting the child,

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:45.260 align:middle line:84%
one of the ways I
tried to convince you

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:47.840 align:middle line:84%
of that was to say,
yeah, not every verb

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.460 align:middle line:90%
can be followed by an object.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:51.570 align:middle line:84%
There are transitive verbs
and intransitive verbs.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.028 align:middle line:84%
That's the kind of
thing that makes

00:24:53.028 --> 00:24:55.010 align:middle line:84%
us think we're looking
at a selection relation.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:58.580 align:middle line:84%
But any verb can be followed
by "with the feather."

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:00.067 align:middle line:84%
You can do anything
with a feather.

00:25:00.067 --> 00:25:01.650 align:middle line:84%
You can tickle a
child with a feather.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:03.020 align:middle line:84%
Or you can devour a
child with a feather.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:04.170 align:middle line:84%
You can write a
novel with a feather.

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:05.545 align:middle line:84%
Or you can thrive
with a feather.

00:25:05.545 --> 00:25:08.090 align:middle line:84%
Feathers, they're just
very, very flexible tools.

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:09.500 align:middle line:90%
You can do anything with them.

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:12.752 align:middle line:84%
Again, some of these things
make more sense than others.

00:25:12.752 --> 00:25:14.960 align:middle line:84%
So it's not clear what you
mean when you say that you

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:17.180 align:middle line:90%
will thrive with a feather.

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:18.480 align:middle line:90%
Or for that matter--

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.120 align:middle line:84%
well, it's actually
fairly clear what

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:22.578 align:middle line:84%
you mean when you say you'll
devour a child with a feather.

00:25:22.578 --> 00:25:26.178 align:middle line:84%
It just doesn't bear
thinking about too closely.

00:25:26.178 --> 00:25:28.220 align:middle line:84%
But basically you can do
anything with a feather.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:31.100 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing like verbs are
transitive and intransitive.

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.020 align:middle line:84%
So this particular
prepositional phrase,

00:25:33.020 --> 00:25:37.320 align:middle line:84%
this sort of instrument, can
go together with anything.

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:41.810 align:middle line:84%
And so we don't seem to want to
say that there is a selection

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:44.000 align:middle line:84%
relation between this
prepositional phrase

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:45.080 align:middle line:90%
and anything at all.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:48.030 align:middle line:90%


00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:50.210 align:middle line:84%
So there's a
distinction that we draw

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:52.602 align:middle line:84%
between what are
called arguments

00:25:52.602 --> 00:25:53.810 align:middle line:90%
and what are called adjuncts.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:57.440 align:middle line:84%
So arguments, like "the child"
in "I will tickle the child

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.530 align:middle line:84%
with a feather," are things
that are selected by something,

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:02.030 align:middle line:90%
in this case, the verb.

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:06.380 align:middle line:84%
And the adjuncts are just
phrases that wandered in.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:08.010 align:middle line:84%
They're not selected
by anything.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.585 align:middle line:84%
They're just there
because they want to be.

00:26:10.585 --> 00:26:11.960 align:middle line:84%
They're not selected
by anything.

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:14.100 align:middle line:84%
You get to put them in
because you feel like it.

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:16.820 align:middle line:84%
They're often modifiers
sort of like this one.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:19.812 align:middle line:90%


00:26:19.812 --> 00:26:20.770 align:middle line:90%
So there are arguments.

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:23.415 align:middle line:90%
And then there are adjuncts.

00:26:23.415 --> 00:26:24.790 align:middle line:84%
How do you tell
whether something

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:26.320 align:middle line:90%
is an argument or an adjunct?

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:29.320 align:middle line:84%
I think I warned you last time
this is something that people

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:30.790 align:middle line:90%
often get confused by.

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:34.450 align:middle line:84%
And so I want to
be clear about it.

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:38.260 align:middle line:84%
Again, when we decided that "the
child" is selected by "tickle,"

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:40.330 align:middle line:84%
what we were doing
was saying not

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:43.430 align:middle line:84%
every verb can be
followed by an object.

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.330 align:middle line:84%
So if you're going to have a
child right after the verb,

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:47.727 align:middle line:84%
you have to know
what verb it is.

00:26:47.727 --> 00:26:49.810 align:middle line:84%
There are transitive verbs
and intransitive verbs.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.940 align:middle line:84%
And some verbs are OK
with the following object,

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.580 align:middle line:90%
and others or not.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:58.520 align:middle line:84%
That's the signature of
a selection relation.

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.700 align:middle line:84%
So the arguments are
picky about which

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:04.000 align:middle line:84%
heads they can combine
with, and adjuncts are not.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.830 align:middle line:84%
What people sometimes
get confused about

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:09.318 align:middle line:84%
is thinking that it's the
opposite, that the pickiness

00:27:09.318 --> 00:27:10.610 align:middle line:90%
goes in the opposite direction.

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:14.440 align:middle line:84%
So people think here's a verb
that needs to have an object.

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And I've probably
said things that

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:17.290 align:middle line:90%
led you down this garden path.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:20.110 align:middle line:84%
So there are indeed verbs that
are obligatorily transitive.

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:22.210 align:middle line:84%
I think the example I
gave before was "devour."

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:31.090 align:middle line:84%
So "The dragon
devoured pizza"---

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:34.270 align:middle line:84%
because we've devoured enough
children for one class,

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:35.200 align:middle line:90%
possibly too many--

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:36.580 align:middle line:90%
"The dragon devoured the pizza."

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:39.670 align:middle line:84%
"Devour" actually has to
combine with an object.

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:42.460 align:middle line:84%
Not only is this a
selection relation

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:44.440 align:middle line:84%
because not just any
verb can have an object,

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:48.040 align:middle line:84%
but actually, "devour" has
to combine with an object.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:50.290 align:middle line:84%
It's ungrammatical
without the pizza.

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:54.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I take out the pizza,
it becomes star, yeah?

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:58.560 align:middle line:84%
So "The dragon devoured,"
that's not a sentence.

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:00.890 align:middle line:84%
So that's an argument, which
is actually obligatory.

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:02.390 align:middle line:90%
"Devour" has to be transitive.

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:05.450 align:middle line:84%
But there are plenty of
verbs that-- in fact, it's

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:09.380 align:middle line:84%
more common for verbs to
be optionally transitive.

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:11.900 align:middle line:84%
So "devour" needs
to have an object.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:16.342 align:middle line:84%
But "eat," which means almost
the same thing as "devour,"

00:28:16.342 --> 00:28:17.550 align:middle line:90%
the transitivity is optional.

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:18.758 align:middle line:90%
You can say "I ate an apple."

00:28:18.758 --> 00:28:21.140 align:middle line:90%
But you can also say "I ate."

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:24.080 align:middle line:84%
Obligatorily transitive verbs
are actually kind of rare.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:26.660 align:middle line:84%
And the best ones
are kind of violent.

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:29.600 align:middle line:84%
They are things like
"eviscerate" and "devour"

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:30.290 align:middle line:90%
and "mutilate."

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:32.420 align:middle line:84%
Those are the really
clear transitive verbs.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:34.760 align:middle line:84%
They're-- very handy
in syntax classes.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:35.300 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:40.046 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So are we suggesting
the fact that since [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:40.046 --> 00:28:44.187 align:middle line:84%
property of a feather, or is
that any adjunct can modify?

00:28:44.187 --> 00:28:46.520 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: We're talking
as though any adjunct can

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:47.780 align:middle line:90%
modify anything.

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:52.950 align:middle line:84%
And so this is another
one of those places where

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:57.690 align:middle line:84%
we have to carefully distinguish
between syntax and semantics.

00:28:57.690 --> 00:28:59.820 align:middle line:84%
There are surely
things that it's

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:03.670 align:middle line:84%
very difficult to imagine
anybody doing with a feather,

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:04.290 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:07.810 align:middle line:90%
But those kinds of cases--

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:11.250 align:middle line:90%
so what's an example?

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:16.950 align:middle line:84%
"She proved Fermat's last
theorem with a feather," right?

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:20.010 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how she
would do that, right?

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:22.320 align:middle line:90%
But that's a math problem.

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:23.770 align:middle line:90%
That's not a syntax problem.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:26.160 align:middle line:90%
So the sentence is grammatical.

00:29:26.160 --> 00:29:28.020 align:middle line:90%
We just can't figure out--

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:29.980 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't be able to
draw a picture of it.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:30.940 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:32.970 align:middle line:84%
So, yeah, what we're saying is
that that prepositional phrase

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:34.920 align:middle line:84%
can combine with
anything in principle.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:37.500 align:middle line:84%
Though there may be
kinds of combinations

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.480 align:middle line:84%
that will give us meanings that
are kind of hard to understand.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:41.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:43.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
stylistically common that

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.070 align:middle line:90%
violent words--

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.070 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: --are
the most transitive ones?

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:48.580 align:middle line:90%
I think it is, yeah.

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:51.000 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there's something about
violence and transitivity,

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:52.473 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:29:52.473 --> 00:29:53.890 align:middle line:84%
Something to think
about as you're

00:29:53.890 --> 00:29:58.090 align:middle line:84%
working on your
languages, if you want to.

00:29:58.090 --> 00:30:00.880 align:middle line:84%
So arguments are picky about
what heads they combined with.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:03.628 align:middle line:84%
But there are--
to look at things

00:30:03.628 --> 00:30:05.170 align:middle line:84%
from the other
perspective, if you're

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:08.980 align:middle line:84%
asking for a particular
head, if you see a head

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:11.140 align:middle line:84%
and it's followed by a
phrase, the question you

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.720 align:middle line:84%
ask yourself is not "Is
that phrase optional?"

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:15.770 align:middle line:90%
People often ask that question.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:18.785 align:middle line:84%
And it's a question that gets
you in the wrong direction.

00:30:18.785 --> 00:30:21.160 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of examples
of things that are, in fact,

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:22.493 align:middle line:90%
arguments, but they're optional.

00:30:22.493 --> 00:30:28.240 align:middle line:90%


00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:31.325 align:middle line:84%
And then I think this is the
last thing we did last time.

00:30:31.325 --> 00:30:33.700 align:middle line:84%
I was trying to illustrate
arguments and adjuncts to you.

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:36.970 align:middle line:84%
And we were in the middle
of deciding on the boat.

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:40.280 align:middle line:84%
So let's decide on the
boat again, fairly quickly.

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.910 align:middle line:84%
So what we decided
was that the sentence

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:46.460 align:middle line:84%
"I decided on the boat" can
mean at least two things.

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:49.180 align:middle line:84%
It can mean either I had
some decision to make,

00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:52.420 align:middle line:84%
and I made it while I
was on the boat, right?

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:54.562 align:middle line:84%
Or it could mean I
chose the boat, right?

00:30:54.562 --> 00:30:57.020 align:middle line:84%
I was trying to choose between
the boat and something else.

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:59.860 align:middle line:90%
And I chose the boat.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:04.960 align:middle line:84%
And what we said was, if we
ask, "The prepositional phrase

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:08.080 align:middle line:84%
'on the boat,' is that an
argument or an adjunct?"

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:11.290 align:middle line:84%
the answer to that
question is yes.

00:31:11.290 --> 00:31:12.580 align:middle line:90%
It can be an adjunct.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:14.950 align:middle line:84%
"I made my decision"
when it has the--

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:16.870 align:middle line:84%
when the sentence means
"I made my decision

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:21.460 align:middle line:84%
while I was on the boat," then
"on the boat" is an adjunct.

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:22.585 align:middle line:90%
You can kind of see why.

00:31:22.585 --> 00:31:24.460 align:middle line:84%
Just like you can do
anything with a feather,

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:26.410 align:middle line:84%
you can do anything
on a boat, right?

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:31.030 align:middle line:84%
So any phrase you can modify
it with "on the boat."

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:32.920 align:middle line:84%
This particular
prepositional phrase,

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:37.730 align:middle line:84%
then, is an adjunct
in that reading, yeah?

00:31:37.730 --> 00:31:41.170 align:middle line:84%
Have to be careful because,
of course, if I say things

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:51.730 align:middle line:84%
like "It depends on the boat,"
or for that-- well, let's use

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:53.322 align:middle line:84%
that one, it
depends on the boat.

00:31:53.322 --> 00:31:55.030 align:middle line:84%
This is a case where
there is a selection

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:58.438 align:middle line:84%
relation between
"depend" and "on," right?

00:31:58.438 --> 00:31:59.980 align:middle line:84%
We've been talking
about that before,

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:02.272 align:middle line:84%
that "depend" select for
prepositional phrase for which

00:32:02.272 --> 00:32:03.400 align:middle line:90%
the head is "on," yeah?

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:05.602 align:middle line:84%
So you can't just look
at a prepositional--

00:32:05.602 --> 00:32:07.810 align:middle line:84%
if someone asks you out of
the blue, is "on the boat"

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:09.313 align:middle line:90%
an argument or an adjunct--

00:32:09.313 --> 00:32:11.230 align:middle line:84%
in a way, that's the
point of this slide too--

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:13.980 align:middle line:84%
the answer is, wait, what's the
rest of the sentence, right?

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:16.450 align:middle line:84%
Because you have to find
out what the relationship is

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:18.658 align:middle line:84%
between that prepositional
phrase and the other words

00:32:18.658 --> 00:32:20.960 align:middle line:90%
that are around it.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:23.770 align:middle line:84%
So in the reading where
"I decided on the boat"

00:32:23.770 --> 00:32:25.840 align:middle line:84%
means "I made my
decision while I

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.000 align:middle line:84%
was on the boat," "on
the boat" is an adjunct.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:29.710 align:middle line:84%
It's just describing
the location

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:32.770 align:middle line:84%
where the rest of the
sentence took place.

00:32:32.770 --> 00:32:36.760 align:middle line:84%
And any sentence can take
place on the boat, maybe.

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:41.290 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to the reading where
it means "I chose the boat,"

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:45.240 align:middle line:84%
there it's an argument
because, again,

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:49.120 align:middle line:84%
just the fact that
"decide on" means choose

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:53.740 align:middle line:84%
is kind of an idiosyncratic
property of the verb "decide"

00:32:53.740 --> 00:32:56.200 align:middle line:90%
and the preposition "on."

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:59.880 align:middle line:84%
It's like if you look up
"decide" in the lexicon,

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:01.540 align:middle line:84%
it's going to have
there, we're going

00:33:01.540 --> 00:33:04.780 align:middle line:84%
to have to list the information,
that it can optionally

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:06.910 align:middle line:84%
select an argument
that starts with "on"

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:10.180 align:middle line:84%
and that the result of that
combination means "choose."

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:11.970 align:middle line:84%
If you think about
other things that

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:13.720 align:middle line:84%
mean something pretty
similar to "decide,"

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:18.430 align:middle line:84%
like "make up your mind," so
"I made up my mind on the boat"

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:21.610 align:middle line:84%
only means the first
thing, I think, right?

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:24.108 align:middle line:84%
"I made up my mind on the
boat" means "I made my decision

00:33:24.108 --> 00:33:25.150 align:middle line:90%
while I was on the boat."

00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:27.100 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean "I
chose the boat," right?

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:29.450 align:middle line:84%
I made a choice between the
boat and something else.

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:31.030 align:middle line:90%
And I chose the boat, right?

00:33:31.030 --> 00:33:33.520 align:middle line:84%
So it's not a property
of expressions

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:36.430 align:middle line:84%
that mean "decide" that they
always combined with "on"

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:37.480 align:middle line:90%
to mean this.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:40.300 align:middle line:84%
It's a idiosyncratic
property of "decide."

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:43.267 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of like we have
to list for particular verbs

00:33:43.267 --> 00:33:45.100 align:middle line:84%
that they're transitive
or that they're not.

00:33:45.100 --> 00:33:46.642 align:middle line:84%
We have to list for
the word "decide"

00:33:46.642 --> 00:33:48.225 align:middle line:84%
that it combines
with "on" to give you

00:33:48.225 --> 00:33:49.780 align:middle line:84%
a meaning with
something like choose.

00:33:49.780 --> 00:33:50.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:51.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm talking over you.

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:52.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this
the same effect

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:53.060 align:middle line:90%
as "look," where you "look up"?

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:54.170 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:33:54.170 --> 00:33:55.590 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's another
good example.

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:59.270 align:middle line:84%
So you can "look
up a reference."

00:33:59.270 --> 00:34:01.880 align:middle line:90%
So you can "look up."

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:05.870 align:middle line:84%
And that "up" is
probably an adjunct.

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:08.150 align:middle line:90%
You can do anything up.

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:10.205 align:middle line:84%
But to "look up a
reference," the fact

00:34:10.205 --> 00:34:14.000 align:middle line:84%
that that means "look through
some stuff and find it,"

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:18.418 align:middle line:84%
that's an idiosyncratic
fact about looking up.

00:34:18.418 --> 00:34:20.210 align:middle line:84%
That's another good
example of an argument.

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:22.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, nice example.

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:23.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:23.840 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:26.590 align:middle line:90%


00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:29.560 align:middle line:84%
And then-- I think this
is where we stopped--

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:32.290 align:middle line:84%
I asked you to think about
sentences like "I decided

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:34.620 align:middle line:90%
on the boat on the plane."

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:38.250 align:middle line:84%
And I asked you to
suppress your natural urge

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:43.239 align:middle line:84%
to make things more complicated
and to think of alternatives.

00:34:43.239 --> 00:34:46.469 align:middle line:84%
So consider situations
in which there's

00:34:46.469 --> 00:34:48.900 align:middle line:84%
only one boat and only one
plane and neither of them

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:51.810 align:middle line:90%
is on the other.

00:34:51.810 --> 00:34:56.370 align:middle line:84%
There are two things that you
could imagine this meaning.

00:34:56.370 --> 00:35:01.140 align:middle line:84%
It could mean I chose the
boat while I was on the plane.

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:03.060 align:middle line:84%
Or it could mean,
in principle, I

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:07.280 align:middle line:84%
chose the plane while
I was on the boat,

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:09.350 align:middle line:84%
if we've decided that
"decide" and "on"

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:11.830 align:middle line:90%
can combine in these ways.

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.890 align:middle line:84%
But I think the fact
generally was that it only

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:16.648 align:middle line:90%
has one of those readings.

00:35:16.648 --> 00:35:18.190 align:middle line:84%
Is that the reading
that people have?

00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:19.750 align:middle line:90%
I'll write the readings down.

00:35:19.750 --> 00:35:31.720 align:middle line:84%
One is chose the boat
while on the plane.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:38.980 align:middle line:84%
And the other was chose the
plane while I was on the boat.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:42.770 align:middle line:90%


00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:43.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:46.340 align:middle line:90%


00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:50.600 align:middle line:84%
How many of you can get it
to mean this first thing?

00:35:50.600 --> 00:35:53.770 align:middle line:84%
How many of you can get it
to mean the second thing?

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:55.807 align:middle line:90%
A few of you.

00:35:55.807 --> 00:35:58.390 align:middle line:84%
The few of you who can get it
to mean the second thing, please

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:00.060 align:middle line:84%
meet with your
classmates after class

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:02.560 align:middle line:84%
and get them to convince you,
or maybe you can convince them

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:04.040 align:middle line:90%
that they're wrong.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:10.330 align:middle line:84%
I think we talked about this a
little bit in class, possibly,

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:14.770 align:middle line:84%
that, for me, at least-- and
people should stop me if this

00:36:14.770 --> 00:36:16.840 align:middle line:90%
is not true--

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:20.230 align:middle line:84%
I can get it to mean
the second thing,

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:22.480 align:middle line:90%
but I need an opera score.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:25.280 align:middle line:84%
I have to say it in
a particular way.

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:29.530 align:middle line:84%
I have to say something like
"I decided, on the boat,

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:30.730 align:middle line:90%
on the plane."

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:32.890 align:middle line:90%
There have to be commas.

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:36.070 align:middle line:84%
And I have to kind of
squash "on the boat"

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:40.790 align:middle line:84%
a little bit in order to get
it to have this second meaning.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.320 align:middle line:84%
Those of you who
were considering

00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:45.110 align:middle line:84%
raising your hands the second
time, is that true of you?

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:48.650 align:middle line:84%
Or are you people who can just
say, "I decided on the boat

00:36:48.650 --> 00:36:51.370 align:middle line:90%
on the plane," and mean this?

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:54.810 align:middle line:90%


00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:57.855 align:middle line:84%
Some of you are raising
thumbs and nodding

00:36:57.855 --> 00:36:58.730 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:36:58.730 --> 00:36:59.720 align:middle line:90%
Is there anybody who--

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:01.720 align:middle line:84%
that thing that I just
said when I pronounced it

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:03.387 align:middle line:84%
in that particular
way, is there anybody

00:37:03.387 --> 00:37:05.502 align:middle line:84%
who did not think, oh,
yeah, that's the way I

00:37:05.502 --> 00:37:07.960 align:middle line:84%
was pronouncing it in my head
when I said that I could have

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:09.880 align:middle line:90%
it be this way?

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:10.730 align:middle line:90%
Let me say it again.

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:13.210 align:middle line:84%
Is there anybody who, if you
say, "I decided on the boat

00:37:13.210 --> 00:37:14.470 align:middle line:90%
on the plane"--

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:15.610 align:middle line:90%
saying it that way--

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:18.730 align:middle line:84%
can it mean this,
the second thing?

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:20.650 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:23.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like that
way, the same sentence is

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:27.810 align:middle line:90%
more neutral than suggesting.

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:29.838 align:middle line:84%
I still have to say
it in a different way

00:37:29.838 --> 00:37:34.090 align:middle line:84%
to get the second meaning,
but when I say like that,

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:36.207 align:middle line:84%
I don't immediately get
the part [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:36.207 --> 00:37:37.040 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:37.873 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:37:37.873 --> 00:37:39.290 align:middle line:84%
How do you have
to say it in order

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:40.462 align:middle line:90%
to get the second reading?

00:37:40.462 --> 00:37:42.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I absolutely
didn't think about that.

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:43.740 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, OK.

00:37:43.740 --> 00:37:44.310 align:middle line:90%
Fair enough.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:45.600 align:middle line:90%
Fair enough.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:48.662 align:middle line:84%
This is one of
many places where I

00:37:48.662 --> 00:37:51.120 align:middle line:84%
think I'm going to be content
with the fact that all of you

00:37:51.120 --> 00:37:53.220 align:middle line:84%
raised your hands when I asked
whether it could mean this

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:54.960 align:middle line:84%
and only a few of you raised
your hands when I asked

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:56.085 align:middle line:90%
whether it could mean this.

00:37:56.085 --> 00:37:58.230 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe the
ones of you who

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:01.050 align:middle line:84%
can get it to mean this are
doing it by pronouncing it

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:03.840 align:middle line:84%
in a especially interesting
way, which is itself

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.340 align:middle line:90%
an interesting fact.

00:38:06.340 --> 00:38:08.610 align:middle line:84%
There is-- I think I have
alluded to this a couple

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:09.720 align:middle line:90%
of times--

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:11.790 align:middle line:84%
a topic of study
that people work on,

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:14.160 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out what
the rules are for how

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.380 align:middle line:84%
the pitch of your voice
rises and falls as you speak

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:18.017 align:middle line:84%
and where you put
in pauses and so on.

00:38:18.017 --> 00:38:20.100 align:middle line:84%
It's the study of what's
sometimes called prosody.

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:24.610 align:middle line:84%
It's very complicated and hard,
but it's really interesting.

00:38:24.610 --> 00:38:28.173 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the short thing for
me to say is, on the prosody

00:38:28.173 --> 00:38:30.090 align:middle line:84%
where there's nothing
special, where we're not

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:33.210 align:middle line:84%
doing any pausing or downgrading
of anything, where we're just

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:34.950 align:middle line:84%
reading this straight
through, I think

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:38.110 align:middle line:90%
everybody prefers this to this.

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:42.440 align:middle line:84%
Let me say that
for the time being.

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:45.520 align:middle line:84%
So "I decided on the
boat on the plane."

00:38:45.520 --> 00:38:48.820 align:middle line:84%
When you've got these two
"on" phrases after "decide,"

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:52.000 align:middle line:84%
the sort of tendency
anyway, unless you

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.107 align:middle line:84%
do fancy prosodic
things, is to have

00:38:54.107 --> 00:38:55.690 align:middle line:84%
the first one be the
argument and then

00:38:55.690 --> 00:38:56.940 align:middle line:90%
the second one be the adjunct.

00:38:56.940 --> 00:39:00.240 align:middle line:90%


00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So what we're finding out, then,
is that if you've got a head

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.510 align:middle line:84%
and it has both an
argument and an adjunct,

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:08.935 align:middle line:84%
the argument is
closer to the head.

00:39:08.935 --> 00:39:10.310 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
come back to that.

00:39:10.310 --> 00:39:13.790 align:middle line:84%
But a way to think about it--
and we'll come back to it

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:15.530 align:middle line:84%
and say this more
formally in a second--

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:16.760 align:middle line:90%
it's as though--

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:19.760 align:middle line:84%
I think I have a tree that
I can make use of here.

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:21.650 align:middle line:84%
It's as though, if
you're going to have

00:39:21.650 --> 00:39:23.900 align:middle line:84%
a verb like "decide"
that's going

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:27.770 align:middle line:84%
to have two prepositional
phrases after it--

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:29.840 align:middle line:84%
and forget what
these are for now,

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:34.370 align:middle line:84%
and also we'll have "on the
boat" and "on the plane."

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:36.950 align:middle line:90%


00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:38.030 align:middle line:90%
So you've got "decide."

00:39:38.030 --> 00:39:40.447 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to have two
prepositional phrases after it.

00:39:40.447 --> 00:39:43.190 align:middle line:84%
And "decide" optionally selects
for a prepositional phrase

00:39:43.190 --> 00:39:45.560 align:middle line:90%
with "on" as the head.

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:48.200 align:middle line:84%
And what we're seeing is
it's as though-- if you

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:49.745 align:middle line:84%
pick the version
of "decide" that's

00:39:49.745 --> 00:39:53.900 align:middle line:84%
like-- if you decide to have
"decide" select for something,

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:57.440 align:middle line:84%
it's as though, when
you're merging these two

00:39:57.440 --> 00:40:00.830 align:middle line:84%
prepositional phrases to
projections of the verb,

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:03.980 align:middle line:84%
you need to merge the one
that's selected first.

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:07.460 align:middle line:84%
There's some kind of urgency
about this selection relation.

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:10.340 align:middle line:84%
You don't just get
to freely decide

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:12.650 align:middle line:84%
what order to merge these
two prepositional phrases in,

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:14.180 align:middle line:90%
the argument and the adjunct.

00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:16.820 align:middle line:84%
You need to hurry up and give
it its selection requirements.

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:18.140 align:middle line:90%
Something like that.

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:20.060 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come back and
talk more about that.

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:24.290 align:middle line:90%


00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:27.380 align:middle line:90%
Incidentally-- well, who cares?

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:32.630 align:middle line:84%
Incidentally, that way
of talking about things--

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:33.770 align:middle line:90%
no, sorry.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.740 align:middle line:84%
Let me wait until we
get more formal about it

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:36.737 align:middle line:90%
to make the next point.

00:40:36.737 --> 00:40:38.570 align:middle line:84%
The next point is that
this may help us some

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:40.460 align:middle line:84%
with the kinds of cases
that got me to write

00:40:40.460 --> 00:40:42.470 align:middle line:84%
that tree in the first
place, the kinds of cases

00:40:42.470 --> 00:40:45.120 align:middle line:84%
where a head seems to select
for more than one thing.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:48.630 align:middle line:90%
So we'll come back to that.

00:40:48.630 --> 00:40:52.010 align:middle line:84%
So that's where we were
last time, I think.

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:54.500 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] Does anybody have any
questions about any of that?

00:40:54.500 --> 00:40:55.490 align:middle line:84%
This has been an
attempt to review

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:57.948 align:middle line:84%
because I know you've had a
week during which you might not

00:40:57.948 --> 00:41:00.680 align:middle line:84%
have spent every waking
moment thinking about syntax.

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:03.770 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why, but I
guess young people today,

00:41:03.770 --> 00:41:06.290 align:middle line:90%
they have their priorities.

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:07.600 align:middle line:90%
Are there questions about this?

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:10.370 align:middle line:84%
Are there things
that aren't clear?

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:11.620 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:14.237 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:41:14.237 --> 00:41:16.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess it seems to
me that "decide" and "decide on"

00:41:16.820 --> 00:41:18.320 align:middle line:84%
are almost like two
different verbs.

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:19.237 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:41:19.237 --> 00:41:21.752 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So why couldn't
you merge "decide" with "on"

00:41:21.752 --> 00:41:24.690 align:middle line:84%
and have that be a verb phrase
and the argument of that

00:41:24.690 --> 00:41:25.763 align:middle line:90%
is [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:25.763 --> 00:41:26.680 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:27.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:27.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:27.850 --> 00:41:32.080 align:middle line:84%
So you're wondering for
"decide on the boat,"

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:38.830 align:middle line:84%
why not do "decide
on" and then combine

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:43.847 align:middle line:84%
that whole thing with a
noun phrase, "the boat,"

00:41:43.847 --> 00:41:44.680 align:middle line:90%
something like that?

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:45.263 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:41:45.263 --> 00:41:46.810 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.210 align:middle line:84%
Notice that these
are making different

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:55.300 align:middle line:84%
claims about whether "on the
boat" is a constituent or not.

00:41:55.300 --> 00:41:57.760 align:middle line:84%
And so, for example,
one of the constituency

00:41:57.760 --> 00:42:02.560 align:middle line:84%
tests that we fooled around
with was the one where

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:06.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm amazed by something
that you've said,

00:42:06.460 --> 00:42:09.140 align:middle line:84%
and I repeat part of what
you've said in amazement.

00:42:09.140 --> 00:42:12.880 align:middle line:84%
So if you say, "I decided on the
boat," one thing I can surely

00:42:12.880 --> 00:42:15.860 align:middle line:90%
say is, "The boat?"

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.630 align:middle line:84%
But I think I can also
say, "On the boat?"

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:20.333 align:middle line:90%


00:42:20.333 --> 00:42:22.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that
switch "up the reference"?

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:24.890 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I
don't think so, right?

00:42:24.890 --> 00:42:27.420 align:middle line:84%
"Up the reference"--
that's a good example.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:32.690 align:middle line:90%
"Look up the reference."

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:36.230 align:middle line:84%
If I'm amazed when you say,
"I looked up the reference,"

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:38.420 align:middle line:90%
if I say, "Up the reference?"

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.670 align:middle line:90%
that's weird.

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:43.410 align:middle line:84%
I have a lifetime's
experience being weird.

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:47.630 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's
worse than "I decided

00:42:47.630 --> 00:42:50.090 align:middle line:90%
on the boat," "On the boat?"

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:50.840 align:middle line:90%
I think.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.585 align:middle line:90%
But, yeah, that's the claim.

00:42:52.585 --> 00:42:53.960 align:middle line:84%
Notice there's
another difference

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:56.335 align:middle line:84%
between "look up the reference"
and "decide on the boat."

00:42:56.335 --> 00:42:59.900 align:middle line:84%
You can also say, "look
the reference up."

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:03.050 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say
"decide the boat on."

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:06.380 align:middle line:84%
So we probably don't want
these to be the same.

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:08.990 align:middle line:84%
It's another reason to think
that they're different.

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:11.600 align:middle line:84%
If you're familiar
with German, German

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:13.400 align:middle line:84%
has a zillion
particles like this,

00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:15.650 align:middle line:84%
where you're putting
things between the verb

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:19.010 align:middle line:84%
and the separable
prefix, they're called.

00:43:19.010 --> 00:43:19.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:23.112 align:middle line:90%


00:43:23.112 --> 00:43:25.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:43:25.930 --> 00:43:28.660 align:middle line:84%
So now, hopefully I've got you
willing to entertain, at least

00:43:28.660 --> 00:43:30.310 align:middle line:84%
temporarily, the
possibility that there

00:43:30.310 --> 00:43:33.790 align:middle line:84%
are things that we can describe
as arguments and adjuncts.

00:43:33.790 --> 00:43:36.550 align:middle line:84%
Arguments are the things that
are selected by other heads.

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:40.330 align:middle line:84%
Adjuncts are phrases that
just wandered in by mistake.

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:41.470 align:middle line:90%
Nothing is selecting them.

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:43.608 align:middle line:84%
They're just here
because they want to be.

00:43:43.608 --> 00:43:45.400 align:middle line:84%
They're not here because
they're satisfying

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:47.072 align:middle line:90%
any needs of anything at all.

00:43:47.072 --> 00:43:49.030 align:middle line:84%
They're parasites,
basically, is what they are.

00:43:49.030 --> 00:43:53.452 align:middle line:84%
They end up in here
inside the clause.

00:43:53.452 --> 00:43:54.910 align:middle line:84%
And I've shown you
one kind of test

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:59.260 align:middle line:84%
for them, which is that if you
have a head that is combining

00:43:59.260 --> 00:44:02.140 align:middle line:84%
both with an argument
and with an adjunct,

00:44:02.140 --> 00:44:05.170 align:middle line:84%
it looks like there's at least
a tendency for the argument

00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:07.880 align:middle line:84%
to want to be closer to
the head than the adjunct.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:10.060 align:middle line:84%
So "decide on the
boat on the plane,"

00:44:10.060 --> 00:44:12.760 align:middle line:84%
the argument-y reading,
the choose reading,

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:14.110 align:middle line:90%
goes with the first one.

00:44:14.110 --> 00:44:17.170 align:middle line:84%
That means chose the
boat on the plane.

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:22.450 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you another test
for arguments versus adjuncts.

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:23.560 align:middle line:90%
Here's another sentence.

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:28.010 align:middle line:84%
"I decided on the boat, and
Mary decided on the plane."

00:44:28.010 --> 00:44:34.650 align:middle line:84%
That can mean at least
two things, I think--

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:37.260 align:middle line:90%
possibly four, but at least two.

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:39.930 align:middle line:84%
One thing that it can
mean is I chose the boat,

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:41.980 align:middle line:90%
and Mary chose the plane.

00:44:41.980 --> 00:44:44.150 align:middle line:84%
Another thing it
can mean is Mary

00:44:44.150 --> 00:44:46.170 align:middle line:84%
and I both had
decisions to make.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:49.302 align:middle line:84%
We had to decide whether to
major in linguistics or not.

00:44:49.302 --> 00:44:51.260 align:middle line:84%
And I made my decision
while I was in the boat,

00:44:51.260 --> 00:44:54.200 align:middle line:84%
and she made hers while
she was on the plane.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:57.320 align:middle line:90%
So it could mean that, too.

00:44:57.320 --> 00:45:00.650 align:middle line:84%
I guess it could also
mean I chose the boat,

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:02.690 align:middle line:84%
and Mary decided to
become a linguistics major

00:45:02.690 --> 00:45:05.150 align:middle line:90%
while she was on the plane?

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:06.525 align:middle line:90%
Maybe?

00:45:06.525 --> 00:45:07.650 align:middle line:90%
I'm not so sure about that.

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:09.733 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, it can mean
at least those two things.

00:45:09.733 --> 00:45:13.790 align:middle line:90%


00:45:13.790 --> 00:45:17.320 align:middle line:90%
Now consider this sentence.

00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:20.185 align:middle line:84%
"I decided on the boat, and
Mary did so on the plane."

00:45:20.185 --> 00:45:24.520 align:middle line:90%


00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:27.900 align:middle line:90%
Does that mean two things?

00:45:27.900 --> 00:45:29.330 align:middle line:90%
I don't think so.

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:30.440 align:middle line:90%
What does that one mean?

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:33.180 align:middle line:90%


00:45:33.180 --> 00:45:33.780 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:34.988 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's the example.

00:45:34.988 --> 00:45:37.370 align:middle line:90%


00:45:37.370 --> 00:45:39.310 align:middle line:84%
I decided to become
a linguistics major

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:40.523 align:middle line:90%
while I was on the boat.

00:45:40.523 --> 00:45:42.730 align:middle line:84%
And Mary chose not to,
but she [INAUDIBLE]..

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:43.540 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, exactly.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:45.248 align:middle line:84%
So I made the right
decision on the boat,

00:45:45.248 --> 00:45:47.170 align:middle line:84%
and Mary made the wrong
decision on the plane.

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:50.440 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it can mean that.

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:50.980 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:53.320 align:middle line:84%
So there's a phenomenon
called VP-pronominalization.

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:59.000 align:middle line:84%
That is you're taking
the VP "decide,"

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:02.350 align:middle line:84%
and you're replacing
it with "do so,"

00:46:02.350 --> 00:46:04.750 align:middle line:90%
which is kind of like a pronoun.

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:07.060 align:middle line:84%
It's like a pronoun in that
it's an expression that

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:09.123 align:middle line:84%
can stand in for lots
of different kinds

00:46:09.123 --> 00:46:09.790 align:middle line:90%
of verb phrases.

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:13.420 align:middle line:84%
So just like a pronoun
like "she" can stand in

00:46:13.420 --> 00:46:16.720 align:middle line:84%
for any female
person, "do so" can

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:20.980 align:middle line:84%
stand in for lots of
kinds of verb phrases.

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:24.910 align:middle line:84%
And what we're learning
is that if you do that,

00:46:24.910 --> 00:46:29.217 align:middle line:84%
the phrase that's outside of
"do so"-- "on the plane"--

00:46:29.217 --> 00:46:32.830 align:middle line:90%


00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:34.120 align:middle line:90%
can only be an adjunct.

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:36.562 align:middle line:90%
It can't be an argument.

00:46:36.562 --> 00:46:38.770 align:middle line:84%
And there are various ways
we could think about this,

00:46:38.770 --> 00:46:40.270 align:middle line:90%
but here's one.

00:46:40.270 --> 00:46:43.495 align:middle line:84%
We could say, look,
this is the argument.

00:46:43.495 --> 00:46:46.790 align:middle line:90%


00:46:46.790 --> 00:46:49.360 align:middle line:90%
This is the adjunct.

00:46:49.360 --> 00:46:52.390 align:middle line:84%
We said there's a special
relation of selection

00:46:52.390 --> 00:46:55.213 align:middle line:84%
between "decide" and
the argument that

00:46:55.213 --> 00:46:57.130 align:middle line:84%
doesn't hold between
"decide" and the adjunct.

00:46:57.130 --> 00:46:59.150 align:middle line:84%
The adjunct is just here
because it wants to be.

00:46:59.150 --> 00:47:01.370 align:middle line:84%
So there's a selection relation
between these two things.

00:47:01.370 --> 00:47:02.800 align:middle line:84%
And then the adjunct
is just something

00:47:02.800 --> 00:47:05.470 align:middle line:84%
you merge because, well, you've
got it, and you can merge it.

00:47:05.470 --> 00:47:08.690 align:middle line:90%


00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:09.940 align:middle line:90%
That's what we've been saying.

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:11.440 align:middle line:84%
And maybe what
we're learning here

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:15.400 align:middle line:84%
is that if you look up
"decide" in the lexicon,

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:17.710 align:middle line:84%
you're going to see that it
has the option of selecting

00:47:17.710 --> 00:47:20.480 align:middle line:84%
for a prepositional
phrase headed by "on."

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:22.930 align:middle line:84%
But if you look up "do
so" in the lexicon,

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:25.270 align:middle line:84%
well, it doesn't
select anything.

00:47:25.270 --> 00:47:28.450 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any selectional
relations with "do so."

00:47:28.450 --> 00:47:31.240 align:middle line:90%
So "do so" is kind of blank.

00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:32.770 align:middle line:84%
It just means there's
a verb phrase,

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:36.520 align:middle line:84%
and look around at the rest
of the sentence to figure out

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:38.380 align:middle line:90%
what the verb phrase is.

00:47:38.380 --> 00:47:40.550 align:middle line:90%
But there's a verb phrase.

00:47:40.550 --> 00:47:42.350 align:middle line:90%
So it's not doing any selecting.

00:47:42.350 --> 00:47:45.670 align:middle line:84%
So if you see a prepositional
phrase after "do so,"

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:48.220 align:middle line:84%
it's not an argument
of anything.

00:47:48.220 --> 00:47:50.110 align:middle line:90%
It had better be an adjunct.

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:52.270 align:middle line:84%
I guess that's what
we're learning here.

00:47:52.270 --> 00:47:53.770 align:middle line:84%
As I told you, this
is another test

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:54.910 align:middle line:90%
for arguments and adjuncts.

00:47:54.910 --> 00:47:58.270 align:middle line:84%
If you do VP-pronominalization--
if you replace the verb phrase

00:47:58.270 --> 00:48:01.300 align:middle line:84%
with "do so" and you
have a phrase left over,

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:03.880 align:middle line:84%
then that thing had
better not be an argument.

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:06.910 align:middle line:90%
It had better be an adjunct.

00:48:06.910 --> 00:48:09.220 align:middle line:84%
The action with
arguments and adjuncts

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:12.340 align:middle line:84%
so far has been with
prepositional phrases.

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:14.770 align:middle line:84%
We've talked as though
direct objects, for example,

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:15.972 align:middle line:90%
are always arguments.

00:48:15.972 --> 00:48:18.430 align:middle line:84%
And so what we expect is that
a direct object will never be

00:48:18.430 --> 00:48:19.722 align:middle line:90%
able to be left over like this.

00:48:19.722 --> 00:48:26.170 align:middle line:84%
That is, you won't be able
to say, "I chose the boat,

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:35.000 align:middle line:84%
and Mary did so the
plane," because "do so"

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:36.230 align:middle line:90%
doesn't select for anything.

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:37.880 align:middle line:84%
Direct objects are
always selected for.

00:48:37.880 --> 00:48:40.190 align:middle line:84%
You get them just if the
verb is a transitive verb.

00:48:40.190 --> 00:48:41.690 align:middle line:84%
And "do so" isn't
a transitive verb.

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:44.420 align:middle line:90%
It's almost not a verb at all.

00:48:44.420 --> 00:48:46.250 align:middle line:84%
Does that accord with
people's intuitions,

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:47.450 align:middle line:90%
that you can't say that?

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:48.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:48:48.050 --> 00:48:48.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:50.570 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I'm going to
star it before anybody

00:48:50.570 --> 00:48:52.880 align:middle line:90%
gets the wrong idea.

00:48:52.880 --> 00:48:53.540 align:middle line:90%
Bad sentence.

00:48:53.540 --> 00:48:54.230 align:middle line:90%
Bad sentence.

00:48:54.230 --> 00:48:55.660 align:middle line:90%
No biscuit!

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:56.658 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:48:56.658 --> 00:48:59.530 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:02.445 align:middle line:90%
All right, so-- yeah?

00:49:02.445 --> 00:49:03.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You
could say, "I chose

00:49:03.820 --> 00:49:05.470 align:middle line:90%
the boat and Mary the plane."

00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:09.025 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yes, good example.

00:49:09.025 --> 00:49:13.759 align:middle line:90%


00:49:13.759 --> 00:49:17.373 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:17.373 --> 00:49:18.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:20.780 align:middle line:84%
So, "I chose a boat
and Mary a plane,"

00:49:20.780 --> 00:49:28.350 align:middle line:84%
or "I decided on a boat
and Mary on a plane."

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:29.060 align:middle line:90%
Can you say that?

00:49:29.060 --> 00:49:33.954 align:middle line:90%


00:49:33.954 --> 00:49:37.373 align:middle line:84%
"I live in Somerville
and Mary in Allston."

00:49:37.373 --> 00:49:38.790 align:middle line:84%
You can have
prepositional phrases

00:49:38.790 --> 00:49:42.040 align:middle line:84%
in this construction, but
maybe not selected ones.

00:49:42.040 --> 00:49:42.540 align:middle line:90%
Ugh.

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:47.242 align:middle line:84%
Because "I decided on the
boat and Mary on the plane,"

00:49:47.242 --> 00:49:48.700 align:middle line:84%
that feels kind of
adjunct-y to me.

00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:50.786 align:middle line:90%
I don't know about guys.

00:49:50.786 --> 00:49:52.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It works,
but it's still vague.

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:55.033 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Works
but it's still vague?

00:49:55.033 --> 00:49:55.950 align:middle line:90%
Is that what you said?

00:49:55.950 --> 00:49:57.180 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:57.180 --> 00:49:58.170 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It
could mean either one?

00:49:58.170 --> 00:49:58.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Either one.

00:49:58.530 --> 00:49:59.400 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK, good.

00:49:59.400 --> 00:50:00.870 align:middle line:84%
I was sort of
hoping that was true

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:02.828 align:middle line:84%
because I don't know any
reason why it wouldn't

00:50:02.828 --> 00:50:04.290 align:middle line:90%
be able to mean either one.

00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:06.133 align:middle line:84%
This is a really
interesting phenomenon.

00:50:06.133 --> 00:50:07.300 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot of work on it.

00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:10.140 align:middle line:90%
It's called gapping.

00:50:10.140 --> 00:50:14.550 align:middle line:84%
And dissertations
have been written.

00:50:14.550 --> 00:50:16.830 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] It's interesting stuff.

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:17.648 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:50:17.648 --> 00:50:19.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you
first read the sentence,

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:23.250 align:middle line:84%
I actually interpreted it as "I
chose the boat and Mary did so

00:50:23.250 --> 00:50:24.110 align:middle line:90%
on the plane."

00:50:24.110 --> 00:50:25.110 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, no.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:26.385 align:middle line:90%
What did Mary do on the plane?

00:50:26.385 --> 00:50:27.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: She chose the boat.

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:29.010 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, she
chose the boat on the plane.

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:29.970 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yes.

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:31.110 align:middle line:90%
Well, that would be OK.

00:50:31.110 --> 00:50:32.475 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] Phew.

00:50:32.475 --> 00:50:34.350 align:middle line:84%
I thought you meant that
she chose the plane.

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:36.930 align:middle line:84%
Yes, no, that's-- no,
that's absolutely right.

00:50:36.930 --> 00:50:38.733 align:middle line:90%
OK, phew.

00:50:38.733 --> 00:50:39.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a nice example.

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:43.950 align:middle line:90%
"I decided on the boat."

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:45.360 align:middle line:90%
Oh, here-- we can use this tree.

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:48.465 align:middle line:84%
"I decided on the boat and
Mary did so on the plane."

00:50:48.465 --> 00:50:51.702 align:middle line:90%


00:50:51.702 --> 00:50:58.020 align:middle line:84%
I was taking that to mean
"decide" in the first clause

00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:00.360 align:middle line:84%
is taking an adjunct,
"on the boat."

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:02.610 align:middle line:84%
And in the second
clause, well, "do so"

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:04.740 align:middle line:90%
is just replacing "decide."

00:51:04.740 --> 00:51:06.690 align:middle line:84%
And "on the plane"
is another adjunct.

00:51:06.690 --> 00:51:09.720 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, yeah, "on the
plane" is an adjunct all right.

00:51:09.720 --> 00:51:12.480 align:middle line:84%
But "do so" is replacing
the entire verb phrase

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:16.770 align:middle line:84%
"decide on the boat," where
"on the boat" is an argument.

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:18.420 align:middle line:84%
And yes, that's
another reading that it

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:20.040 align:middle line:90%
ought to be able to have.

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:21.780 align:middle line:84%
I was just ignoring
that reading.

00:51:21.780 --> 00:51:23.430 align:middle line:84%
Thank you for pulling
that reading out

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:24.960 align:middle line:84%
and making me pay
attention to it.

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:26.770 align:middle line:90%
You're absolutely right.

00:51:26.770 --> 00:51:27.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:28.330 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:29.200 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:51:29.200 --> 00:51:33.100 align:middle line:90%
So arguments versus adjuncts--

00:51:33.100 --> 00:51:35.080 align:middle line:84%
"decide," "on the
boat," "on the plane."

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:37.420 align:middle line:90%
"On the boat" is an argument.

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:40.360 align:middle line:84%
This means I chose the
boat on the plane, at least

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:41.860 align:middle line:90%
on the most natural prosody.

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:47.710 align:middle line:84%
And we've now seen two ways
of distinguishing arguments

00:51:47.710 --> 00:51:48.310 align:middle line:90%
from adjuncts.

00:51:48.310 --> 00:51:51.460 align:middle line:84%
One is if you have
anything left over after

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:53.230 align:middle line:90%
VP-pronominalization--

00:51:53.230 --> 00:51:55.030 align:middle line:84%
anything that's
outside, "do so."

00:51:55.030 --> 00:51:56.740 align:middle line:84%
Anything that's still
there had better

00:51:56.740 --> 00:51:59.290 align:middle line:84%
be an adjunct and not an
argument because "do so"

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:02.090 align:middle line:90%
doesn't select for things.

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:05.050 align:middle line:84%
And if you have both an
argument and an adjunct,

00:52:05.050 --> 00:52:09.460 align:middle line:84%
the argument tends to be closer
to the verb than the adjunct.

00:52:09.460 --> 00:52:12.070 align:middle line:84%
Those are the two tests
that I've given you so far.

00:52:12.070 --> 00:52:17.970 align:middle line:90%


00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:21.085 align:middle line:84%
And then here's a
mini-constituency test

00:52:21.085 --> 00:52:23.460 align:middle line:84%
that's meant to get you to
take seriously the possibility

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:25.680 align:middle line:84%
that there is a constituent,
"decide on the boat."

00:52:25.680 --> 00:52:28.560 align:middle line:84%
Look, you can coordinate
that constituent

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:30.330 align:middle line:84%
with another constituent
of the same kind.

00:52:30.330 --> 00:52:32.100 align:middle line:84%
You can say, "Mary
will decide on the boat

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:34.075 align:middle line:90%
and read a novel on the plane."

00:52:34.075 --> 00:52:34.575 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:52:34.575 --> 00:52:37.680 align:middle line:90%


00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:41.610 align:middle line:84%
So, terminology break-- the
lower thing is the complement.

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:43.860 align:middle line:90%
It's the sister of the head.

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:45.840 align:middle line:84%
So "complement"
is a name that we

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:48.190 align:middle line:84%
use for sisters of heads
which, as we've seen,

00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:52.980 align:middle line:84%
is a position that's kind
of privileged for arguments,

00:52:52.980 --> 00:52:54.250 align:middle line:90%
for things that are selected.

00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:59.070 align:middle line:84%
So if there's only going
to be one thing that

00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:01.860 align:middle line:84%
can be-- if there's a
competition between an argument

00:53:01.860 --> 00:53:03.663 align:middle line:84%
and an adjunct to
be the complement,

00:53:03.663 --> 00:53:05.580 align:middle line:84%
well, it's the argument
that's the complement.

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:07.538 align:middle line:84%
That's the thing that
gets to be the sister.

00:53:07.538 --> 00:53:08.580 align:middle line:90%
That's what we're seeing.

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:12.770 align:middle line:90%


00:53:12.770 --> 00:53:15.940 align:middle line:84%
So here's the principle
that I introduced informally

00:53:15.940 --> 00:53:18.550 align:middle line:84%
a second ago and I'll now
say a little more formally.

00:53:18.550 --> 00:53:21.310 align:middle line:84%
If you've got a head that's
selecting for an argument, what

00:53:21.310 --> 00:53:25.930 align:middle line:84%
we're seeing is you need to
take care of the selection

00:53:25.930 --> 00:53:29.470 align:middle line:84%
requirements of the head first
before you do anything else.

00:53:29.470 --> 00:53:31.900 align:middle line:84%
You must first eat your
broccoli before you

00:53:31.900 --> 00:53:33.130 align:middle line:90%
can have your dessert.

00:53:33.130 --> 00:53:34.810 align:middle line:84%
You have to take
your core classes

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:37.540 align:middle line:84%
before you can switch to
taking all linguistics classes.

00:53:37.540 --> 00:53:40.670 align:middle line:90%
That's the way life is.

00:53:40.670 --> 00:53:43.630 align:middle line:84%
So if you're going to
read books quickly,

00:53:43.630 --> 00:53:45.430 align:middle line:90%
"read" is a transitive verb.

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:49.210 align:middle line:84%
It can combine
with a noun phrase.

00:53:49.210 --> 00:53:52.930 align:middle line:84%
And so you're going to want to
give it its noun phrase first.

00:53:52.930 --> 00:53:55.540 align:middle line:84%
That's the first thing
you'll merge with "read."

00:53:55.540 --> 00:53:56.950 align:middle line:90%
"Quickly" is an adjunct.

00:53:56.950 --> 00:53:58.720 align:middle line:90%
You can do anything quickly.

00:53:58.720 --> 00:54:00.460 align:middle line:84%
It's not getting
selected by "read."

00:54:00.460 --> 00:54:02.860 align:middle line:84%
We won't find verbs that
can combine with "quickly"

00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:03.940 align:middle line:90%
and verbs that can't.

00:54:03.940 --> 00:54:08.380 align:middle line:84%
And so, yeah, you need to
satisfy the needs of "read"

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:11.200 align:middle line:84%
first before you go merging
"quickly," which is just

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:13.160 align:middle line:90%
kind of an optional thing.

00:54:13.160 --> 00:54:15.760 align:middle line:84%
And that's why the
story is going to be--

00:54:15.760 --> 00:54:18.160 align:middle line:84%
we can say, "He read
the book quickly,"

00:54:18.160 --> 00:54:21.580 align:middle line:84%
but not, "He read quickly
the book," in English.

00:54:21.580 --> 00:54:22.780 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:54:22.780 --> 00:54:25.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In languages that
have flexible word order--

00:54:25.150 --> 00:54:26.370 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:54:26.370 --> 00:54:27.760 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] Let me sit down.

00:54:27.760 --> 00:54:29.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do
these sentences--

00:54:29.920 --> 00:54:34.420 align:middle line:84%
the sentence trees work
out if you can rearrange?

00:54:34.420 --> 00:54:37.252 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So you're
pointing to the fact

00:54:37.252 --> 00:54:39.460 align:middle line:84%
that there are many languages
out there in which word

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:42.940 align:middle line:84%
order is a lot more flexible
than it is in English.

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:44.920 align:middle line:90%
And you know what?

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:47.140 align:middle line:84%
Even in English,
it sometimes gets

00:54:47.140 --> 00:54:48.680 align:middle line:90%
more complicated than this.

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:52.060 align:middle line:84%
So let me show you the ways in
which it gets more complicated

00:54:52.060 --> 00:54:52.930 align:middle line:90%
in English.

00:54:52.930 --> 00:54:59.020 align:middle line:84%
And when we get to that,
we will have a tool

00:54:59.020 --> 00:55:00.610 align:middle line:84%
that we can use
to describe what's

00:55:00.610 --> 00:55:03.670 align:middle line:84%
going on in the languages
you have in mind.

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:07.090 align:middle line:84%
So let me just get you to hold
off on your question for now.

00:55:07.090 --> 00:55:10.810 align:middle line:84%
Are there any other
questions about this so far?

00:55:10.810 --> 00:55:11.590 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:11.590 --> 00:55:12.090 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:55:12.090 --> 00:55:14.700 align:middle line:90%


00:55:14.700 --> 00:55:17.010 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I think we might
be about to do it right now.

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:17.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:55:17.510 --> 00:55:18.870 align:middle line:90%
So where are we?

00:55:18.870 --> 00:55:20.990 align:middle line:84%
Trees are constructed
by binary Merge.

00:55:20.990 --> 00:55:23.817 align:middle line:84%
Merge is constrained by
selection via this thing I just

00:55:23.817 --> 00:55:25.650 align:middle line:84%
called the Projection
Principle, which says,

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:27.330 align:middle line:84%
if a head selects
for something, then

00:55:27.330 --> 00:55:29.910 align:middle line:84%
that should be the
first thing you merge.

00:55:29.910 --> 00:55:31.970 align:middle line:84%
Before, some of you
were tormenting me

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:33.720 align:middle line:84%
with questions about
heads that seem to be

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:35.190 align:middle line:90%
selecting more than one thing.

00:55:35.190 --> 00:55:37.440 align:middle line:84%
And you can kind of interpret
the Projection Principle

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:39.450 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's
sympathetic to that.

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:41.550 align:middle line:84%
You get to say, yeah,
if you have a head that

00:55:41.550 --> 00:55:44.760 align:middle line:84%
selects for more than one
thing, "give the children books"

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:49.440 align:middle line:84%
or "put the book on the
table," it has two needs.

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:51.300 align:middle line:84%
And you had better
satisfy those needs

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.210 align:middle line:84%
as fast as you can,
modulo binary merge.

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:56.227 align:middle line:90%
So give it one of its arguments.

00:55:56.227 --> 00:55:57.810 align:middle line:84%
And then it still
has another argument

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:00.630 align:middle line:84%
it needs, so you better
merge that one next.

00:56:00.630 --> 00:56:03.810 align:middle line:84%
The tools that we have right
now make that a possible answer

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:06.750 align:middle line:90%
to those kinds of questions.

00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:08.993 align:middle line:84%
Questions still floating
around about whether those

00:56:08.993 --> 00:56:10.410 align:middle line:84%
are the right
trees, but those are

00:56:10.410 --> 00:56:12.660 align:middle line:90%
the trees we expect right now.

00:56:12.660 --> 00:56:14.120 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:56:14.120 --> 00:56:15.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:56:15.460 --> 00:56:16.400 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:56:16.400 --> 00:56:18.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:56:18.130 --> 00:56:18.630 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:56:18.630 --> 00:56:20.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So for a
verb like "give,"

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:22.880 align:middle line:84%
and you are trying to make
a construction like "give

00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:27.450 align:middle line:84%
the children books,"
would it make sense

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:30.090 align:middle line:84%
to instead say that
"books" as an argument

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:36.850 align:middle line:84%
is an argument for what you
get after you merge "gives"

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:39.240 align:middle line:90%
with that first argument?

00:56:39.240 --> 00:56:41.400 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of like--

00:56:41.400 --> 00:56:43.200 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
I see what you mean.

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:43.700 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:45.696 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:56:45.696 --> 00:56:48.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Kind of like
cutting in lambda calculus.

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:49.795 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:56:49.795 --> 00:56:50.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes, it is like that.

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:53.170 align:middle line:84%
That's a nice way
to talk about it.

00:56:53.170 --> 00:56:57.288 align:middle line:90%
So I've been talking as though--

00:56:57.288 --> 00:57:00.300 align:middle line:84%
let's see if I can
say it coherently.

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:04.590 align:middle line:84%
I've been talking as though
when we look up "give," "give"

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:07.273 align:middle line:84%
is going to say, well,
I need two noun phrases.

00:57:07.273 --> 00:57:08.940 align:middle line:84%
Or at least there is
a version of "give"

00:57:08.940 --> 00:57:10.710 align:middle line:90%
that takes two noun phrases.

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:14.708 align:middle line:84%
You're asking me,
wait, couldn't--

00:57:14.708 --> 00:57:17.250 align:middle line:84%
first of all, don't we want to
say something more complicated

00:57:17.250 --> 00:57:17.880 align:middle line:90%
than that?

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:19.657 align:middle line:84%
And we saw this
with "put" as well.

00:57:19.657 --> 00:57:21.240 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't just want
two noun phrases.

00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:23.760 align:middle line:84%
It wants two noun phrases
in a particular order.

00:57:23.760 --> 00:57:25.920 align:middle line:84%
So you want to "give
the children books."

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:29.070 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to "give
the books children,"

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:31.770 align:middle line:90%
meaning give children books.

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:34.860 align:middle line:84%
So the first noun
phrase in this list

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:36.810 align:middle line:84%
is the noun phrase
that gets the books,

00:57:36.810 --> 00:57:37.980 align:middle line:90%
gets the second noun phrase.

00:57:37.980 --> 00:57:39.760 align:middle line:90%
It's not the other way around.

00:57:39.760 --> 00:57:42.070 align:middle line:84%
And in "put the
book on the table,"

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:44.010 align:middle line:84%
"put" maybe selects
for a noun phrase

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:48.082 align:middle line:84%
and a prepositional
phrase, but we

00:57:48.082 --> 00:57:50.040 align:middle line:84%
need to say something
more structured than that

00:57:50.040 --> 00:57:51.990 align:middle line:84%
because it has to be the
noun phrase first and then

00:57:51.990 --> 00:57:52.800 align:middle line:90%
the prepositional phrase.

00:57:52.800 --> 00:57:54.280 align:middle line:84%
It can't be the
other way around.

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And you're wondering, can
we say something like,

00:57:57.660 --> 00:58:02.790 align:middle line:84%
"give" is a function
that takes a noun phrase

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:06.840 align:middle line:84%
and gets converted into another
function that also takes a noun

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:08.295 align:middle line:90%
phrase, something like that.

00:58:08.295 --> 00:58:11.042 align:middle line:90%


00:58:11.042 --> 00:58:12.750 align:middle line:84%
That's sort of the
equivalent of currying

00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:17.490 align:middle line:84%
in or Schönfinkelization
or whatever.

00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:19.890 align:middle line:84%
If you ever study
semantics in my department,

00:58:19.890 --> 00:58:22.940 align:middle line:84%
I have several German
semantics colleagues,

00:58:22.940 --> 00:58:24.690 align:middle line:84%
colleagues whose native
language is German

00:58:24.690 --> 00:58:26.520 align:middle line:90%
and they are semanticists.

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:31.787 align:middle line:84%
And there were two people who
invented the kind of functions

00:58:31.787 --> 00:58:33.870 align:middle line:84%
that he's talking about,
where you have a function

00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:36.120 align:middle line:84%
and it changes the function
into another function that

00:58:36.120 --> 00:58:38.200 align:middle line:90%
takes another kind of thing.

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:40.440 align:middle line:84%
One of them had
the surname Curry,

00:58:40.440 --> 00:58:42.865 align:middle line:84%
and the other had the
surname Schönfinkel.

00:58:42.865 --> 00:58:45.720 align:middle line:90%


00:58:45.720 --> 00:58:47.340 align:middle line:90%
This guy was, I think, British.

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:49.170 align:middle line:90%
And this person was German.

00:58:49.170 --> 00:58:52.660 align:middle line:84%
So most English speakers
refer to this as currying in.

00:58:52.660 --> 00:58:55.950 align:middle line:84%
But if you are German, you refer
to it as Schönfinkelization.

00:58:55.950 --> 00:58:56.910 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:56.910 --> 00:59:00.360 align:middle line:84%
And apparently Schönfinkel
discovered it before Curry,

00:59:00.360 --> 00:59:03.420 align:middle line:84%
so the Germans are annoyed
by the fact that we call it

00:59:03.420 --> 00:59:04.502 align:middle line:90%
currying.

00:59:04.502 --> 00:59:06.210 align:middle line:84%
I'm not German, so
I'm not annoyed by you

00:59:06.210 --> 00:59:08.580 align:middle line:90%
calling it currying in.

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:10.950 align:middle line:84%
Indeed, we clearly want
something more structured

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:12.600 align:middle line:90%
than what I'm giving us.

00:59:12.600 --> 00:59:15.645 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just going to leave
your point right there.

00:59:15.645 --> 00:59:17.400 align:middle line:84%
We need to do something
more sophisticated

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:19.578 align:middle line:90%
than what I'm doing here.

00:59:19.578 --> 00:59:21.120 align:middle line:84%
I keep hinting at
the fact that we're

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:24.120 align:middle line:84%
going to want to investigate
these trees, the trees that I'm

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:26.160 align:middle line:84%
showing you, with
other techniques

00:59:26.160 --> 00:59:30.390 align:middle line:84%
to find out whether these
are the right constituency

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:31.270 align:middle line:90%
structures.

00:59:31.270 --> 00:59:33.660 align:middle line:84%
And when we do
that, I think we'll

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:36.743 align:middle line:84%
have another reason to revise
what I'm telling you now.

00:59:36.743 --> 00:59:38.910 align:middle line:84%
So there are several reasons
to be uneasy about what

00:59:38.910 --> 00:59:40.920 align:middle line:90%
I'm telling you now.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.770 align:middle line:84%
I've shown you a theory
which works fine as long as

00:59:43.770 --> 00:59:46.032 align:middle line:90%
heads only have one argument.

00:59:46.032 --> 00:59:47.490 align:middle line:84%
As soon as they
have two arguments,

00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:50.538 align:middle line:84%
these kinds of unsettling
questions arise.

00:59:50.538 --> 00:59:52.080 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
come back to them.

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:53.630 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:59:53.630 --> 00:59:54.960 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:56.790 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:59:56.790 --> 00:59:57.870 align:middle line:90%
This is great.

00:59:57.870 --> 01:00:01.140 align:middle line:84%
I feel like I'm in
a graduate seminar.

01:00:01.140 --> 01:00:02.150 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:00:02.150 --> 01:00:02.810 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:00:02.810 --> 01:00:05.210 align:middle line:90%
So this is where we are then.

01:00:05.210 --> 01:00:07.145 align:middle line:84%
Trees are constructed
by binary Merge.

01:00:07.145 --> 01:00:09.020 align:middle line:84%
We have the Projection
Principle, which says,

01:00:09.020 --> 01:00:10.220 align:middle line:84%
if there's a selection
relation, you

01:00:10.220 --> 01:00:11.780 align:middle line:84%
need to do that
Merge relation first.

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:14.690 align:middle line:90%


01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:17.570 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to get us
contrasts like "Mary wrote

01:00:17.570 --> 01:00:19.785 align:middle line:84%
the novel on a
typewriter" versus "Mary

01:00:19.785 --> 01:00:22.160 align:middle line:84%
wrote on a typewriter the
novel," where the second one is

01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:26.270 align:middle line:84%
no good because "write"
has the option of being

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:27.560 align:middle line:90%
a transitive verb.

01:00:27.560 --> 01:00:32.000 align:middle line:84%
And when it's a transitive verb,
it selects for a direct object.

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:33.590 align:middle line:90%
And that's the novel.

01:00:33.590 --> 01:00:35.990 align:middle line:84%
"On a typewriter" is
like "with a feather."

01:00:35.990 --> 01:00:37.520 align:middle line:84%
You can do anything
on a typewriter.

01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:38.880 align:middle line:90%
You can combine with anything.

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:39.830 align:middle line:90%
So it's an adjunct.

01:00:39.830 --> 01:00:42.110 align:middle line:84%
And so there's no
hurry to merge it.

01:00:42.110 --> 01:00:47.072 align:middle line:90%


01:00:47.072 --> 01:00:48.780 align:middle line:84%
So we've worked our
way to the conclusion

01:00:48.780 --> 01:00:51.150 align:middle line:84%
that "write," when it's
transitive, absolutely,

01:00:51.150 --> 01:00:53.792 align:middle line:84%
absolutely has to
select an object.

01:00:53.792 --> 01:00:56.250 align:middle line:84%
And the object has to be the
first thing you merge together

01:00:56.250 --> 01:00:57.360 align:middle line:90%
with "write."

01:00:57.360 --> 01:00:59.772 align:middle line:84%
So it has to be right
after "write" in English.

01:00:59.772 --> 01:01:01.230 align:middle line:84%
That's why you can
say, "Mary wrote

01:01:01.230 --> 01:01:02.355 align:middle line:90%
the novel on a typewriter."

01:01:02.355 --> 01:01:05.112 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say "Mary wrote
on a typewriter the novel."

01:01:05.112 --> 01:01:06.570 align:middle line:84%
The novel, the
thing that's getting

01:01:06.570 --> 01:01:08.895 align:middle line:84%
written, it absolutely has
to be right next to "write."

01:01:08.895 --> 01:01:11.850 align:middle line:90%


01:01:11.850 --> 01:01:13.770 align:middle line:90%
Darn!

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:18.270 align:middle line:84%
That last example looks
problematic, right?

01:01:18.270 --> 01:01:21.510 align:middle line:84%
If I ask you, "What did
Mary write on a typewriter,"

01:01:21.510 --> 01:01:24.300 align:middle line:84%
we've just worked our way to
the conclusion that "write,"

01:01:24.300 --> 01:01:26.308 align:middle line:84%
when it takes a direct
object, the direct object

01:01:26.308 --> 01:01:28.350 align:middle line:84%
has to be the first thing
you Merge with "write."

01:01:28.350 --> 01:01:29.808 align:middle line:84%
It has to be right
next to "write."

01:01:29.808 --> 01:01:32.010 align:middle line:84%
There can't be anything
intervening between "write"

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:36.840 align:middle line:84%
and it's complement, the
thing that's getting written.

01:01:36.840 --> 01:01:39.960 align:middle line:84%
What's the thing that's getting
written in that example?

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:41.820 align:middle line:84%
What did Mary write
on the typewriter?

01:01:41.820 --> 01:01:42.510 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:42.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "What."

01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:43.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "What."

01:01:43.780 --> 01:01:45.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:47.648 align:middle line:84%
This sounds like an Abbott
and Costello routine.

01:01:47.648 --> 01:01:48.690 align:middle line:90%
"What's getting written."

01:01:48.690 --> 01:01:50.070 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:01:50.070 --> 01:01:50.820 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:50.820 --> 01:01:52.740 align:middle line:90%


01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:55.320 align:middle line:84%
But that's not the
sister of "write."

01:01:55.320 --> 01:01:56.520 align:middle line:90%
That's nowhere near "write."

01:01:56.520 --> 01:01:58.680 align:middle line:84%
It's way the heck at the
beginning of the sentence.

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:02.000 align:middle line:90%
What the heck is going on?

01:02:02.000 --> 01:02:03.942 align:middle line:84%
Are you all
emotionally distraught?

01:02:03.942 --> 01:02:05.150 align:middle line:90%
That's what I'm trying to do.

01:02:05.150 --> 01:02:05.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:02:05.650 --> 01:02:07.670 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:02:07.670 --> 01:02:11.450 align:middle line:84%
So two possible
responses-- oh, well, we

01:02:11.450 --> 01:02:13.730 align:middle line:84%
didn't have time to get too
attached to the Projection

01:02:13.730 --> 01:02:14.438 align:middle line:90%
Principle anyway.

01:02:14.438 --> 01:02:16.590 align:middle line:84%
It was just a couple
of slides ago.

01:02:16.590 --> 01:02:18.200 align:middle line:90%
So much for that idea.

01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:19.420 align:middle line:90%
That's one possibility.

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:21.698 align:middle line:84%
So apparently heads
can select for things,

01:02:21.698 --> 01:02:23.490 align:middle line:84%
and they don't have to
be anywhere near it.

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:25.020 align:middle line:90%
So, well.

01:02:25.020 --> 01:02:25.520 align:middle line:90%
But, look.

01:02:25.520 --> 01:02:26.645 align:middle line:90%
Here's another possibility.

01:02:26.645 --> 01:02:30.860 align:middle line:84%
We could say, no, the
Projection Principle is right--

01:02:30.860 --> 01:02:32.840 align:middle line:90%
is correct.

01:02:32.840 --> 01:02:35.360 align:middle line:84%
I should have picked
a different verb.

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:37.140 align:middle line:84%
The Projection
Principle is correct.

01:02:37.140 --> 01:02:38.660 align:middle line:84%
It is true that a
verb like "write"

01:02:38.660 --> 01:02:40.400 align:middle line:84%
that selects for an
object absolutely has

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:41.692 align:middle line:90%
to merge with the object first.

01:02:41.692 --> 01:02:45.010 align:middle line:84%
And so, really what
you're doing when

01:02:45.010 --> 01:02:47.350 align:middle line:84%
you start to ask a question
like, "What did Mary

01:02:47.350 --> 01:02:48.820 align:middle line:84%
write on a
typewriter?" is you are

01:02:48.820 --> 01:02:50.635 align:middle line:90%
merging "write" with "what."

01:02:50.635 --> 01:02:53.020 align:middle line:84%
"What" is starting off
as the sister of "write."

01:02:53.020 --> 01:02:55.810 align:middle line:84%
So you start off with something
more like "Mary wrote what

01:02:55.810 --> 01:02:59.110 align:middle line:90%
on a typewriter."

01:02:59.110 --> 01:03:01.630 align:middle line:84%
And then, apparently,
there is some operation

01:03:01.630 --> 01:03:04.260 align:middle line:84%
that takes "what" and
puts it somewhere else,

01:03:04.260 --> 01:03:05.635 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning
of the sentence.

01:03:05.635 --> 01:03:10.780 align:middle line:90%


01:03:10.780 --> 01:03:14.590 align:middle line:90%
So-- this was a while ago--

01:03:14.590 --> 01:03:17.050 align:middle line:84%
Kateryna asked me,
what about languages

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:18.730 align:middle line:90%
where word order is freer?

01:03:18.730 --> 01:03:21.640 align:middle line:84%
And I said, things are about
to get more complicated.

01:03:21.640 --> 01:03:24.110 align:middle line:84%
Things have now gotten
more complicated.

01:03:24.110 --> 01:03:25.900 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah, there's selection.

01:03:25.900 --> 01:03:27.070 align:middle line:90%
Things select for things.

01:03:27.070 --> 01:03:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And when x selects
for y, then y needs

01:03:29.920 --> 01:03:31.780 align:middle line:90%
to merge with x right away.

01:03:31.780 --> 01:03:34.330 align:middle line:84%
And there are adjuncts that
can merge wherever they want.

01:03:34.330 --> 01:03:36.460 align:middle line:90%
And there are things like this--

01:03:36.460 --> 01:03:38.440 align:middle line:90%
movement operations.

01:03:38.440 --> 01:03:41.150 align:middle line:84%
I can spend some time trying to
convince you that this is true.

01:03:41.150 --> 01:03:44.350 align:middle line:84%
But right now let me just assert
that it's true-- cases where,

01:03:44.350 --> 01:03:48.008 align:middle line:84%
indeed, things do merge just the
way we've convinced ourselves

01:03:48.008 --> 01:03:48.550 align:middle line:90%
that they do.

01:03:48.550 --> 01:03:49.758 align:middle line:90%
They merge where they should.

01:03:49.758 --> 01:03:51.400 align:middle line:90%
They merge as sisters of verbs.

01:03:51.400 --> 01:03:56.080 align:middle line:84%
But then something else happens,
and they end up somewhere else.

01:03:56.080 --> 01:03:58.000 align:middle line:84%
So there's this
operation of movement

01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:00.370 align:middle line:84%
where you take the word
"what," and you move it

01:04:00.370 --> 01:04:03.160 align:middle line:84%
to the beginning of the sentence
in order to form questions

01:04:03.160 --> 01:04:04.480 align:middle line:90%
like this one.

01:04:04.480 --> 01:04:07.450 align:middle line:84%
Notice that in English, and
indeed in many languages--

01:04:07.450 --> 01:04:08.290 align:middle line:90%
most languages.

01:04:08.290 --> 01:04:11.290 align:middle line:84%
Not all languages,
but in many languages,

01:04:11.290 --> 01:04:15.580 align:middle line:84%
if I'm amazed by
something you've

01:04:15.580 --> 01:04:20.920 align:middle line:84%
said-- if you say, "Mary wrote
a proof of Fermat's Last Theorem

01:04:20.920 --> 01:04:23.710 align:middle line:84%
on a typewriter,"
I can say, "Mary

01:04:23.710 --> 01:04:25.660 align:middle line:90%
wrote what on the typewriter?"

01:04:25.660 --> 01:04:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So it actually is possible to
say these words in this order.

01:04:28.330 --> 01:04:31.180 align:middle line:84%
I just have to be in a
particular emotional state.

01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:32.995 align:middle line:90%
I have to be astonished.

01:04:32.995 --> 01:04:35.170 align:middle line:84%
This is actually
cross-linguistically extremely

01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:36.910 align:middle line:90%
common for--

01:04:36.910 --> 01:04:39.340 align:middle line:84%
they're called echo
questions, where,

01:04:39.340 --> 01:04:41.933 align:middle line:84%
if I can't believe
what you just said

01:04:41.933 --> 01:04:44.350 align:middle line:84%
or if I couldn't quite hear
what you just said-- if you're

01:04:44.350 --> 01:04:47.710 align:middle line:84%
telling me on the
T, and you say,

01:04:47.710 --> 01:04:50.047 align:middle line:84%
"Mary wrote [GARGLING NOISE]
on a typewriter,"

01:04:50.047 --> 01:04:51.880 align:middle line:84%
and there's lots of
noise in the background,

01:04:51.880 --> 01:04:53.960 align:middle line:84%
and I missed the crucial
thing, I can say,

01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:55.997 align:middle line:84%
"Mary wrote what
on a typewriter?"

01:04:55.997 --> 01:04:57.580 align:middle line:84%
These are called
echo questions, where

01:04:57.580 --> 01:05:01.300 align:middle line:84%
I'm repeating most
of what you just said

01:05:01.300 --> 01:05:04.150 align:middle line:84%
and replacing part
of it with a wh-word.

01:05:04.150 --> 01:05:06.100 align:middle line:84%
And it's extremely common
cross-linguistically

01:05:06.100 --> 01:05:09.100 align:middle line:84%
for it to be possible
to leave these question

01:05:09.100 --> 01:05:12.077 align:middle line:84%
words like "what"
in the positions

01:05:12.077 --> 01:05:14.660 align:middle line:84%
where you would expect them to
be in those kinds of questions,

01:05:14.660 --> 01:05:16.577 align:middle line:84%
in the positions where
they would be selected.

01:05:16.577 --> 01:05:18.062 align:middle line:90%
It's not universal, actually.

01:05:18.062 --> 01:05:20.020 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
don't let you do that.

01:05:20.020 --> 01:05:22.640 align:middle line:84%
If you're working
on a language, it

01:05:22.640 --> 01:05:24.760 align:middle line:84%
could be interesting
to do some finding out

01:05:24.760 --> 01:05:29.430 align:middle line:84%
about how it forms these
kinds of questions.

01:05:29.430 --> 01:05:32.450 align:middle line:84%
OK, so lots of
questions about what's

01:05:32.450 --> 01:05:34.910 align:middle line:90%
going on in this last example.

01:05:34.910 --> 01:05:36.920 align:middle line:84%
There are several
reasons to think

01:05:36.920 --> 01:05:40.670 align:middle line:90%
that "what" is ending up in CP.

01:05:40.670 --> 01:05:41.750 align:middle line:90%
Remember CP?

01:05:41.750 --> 01:05:47.300 align:middle line:84%
It was the thing that had heads
like "whether" and "that."

01:05:47.300 --> 01:05:50.420 align:middle line:84%
And we said verbs like
"know" and "think"--

01:05:50.420 --> 01:05:52.700 align:middle line:84%
and "wonder" I think was
the example I used before--

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:54.740 align:middle line:90%
they select for clauses.

01:05:54.740 --> 01:05:56.900 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, they
select for properties

01:05:56.900 --> 01:05:58.200 align:middle line:90%
of the head of the clause.

01:05:58.200 --> 01:06:02.525 align:middle line:84%
So "think" can select for a
clause that's headed by "that."

01:06:02.525 --> 01:06:03.800 align:middle line:90%
"That" is the complementizer.

01:06:03.800 --> 01:06:06.410 align:middle line:84%
That's our word
for it in syntax.

01:06:06.410 --> 01:06:08.300 align:middle line:84%
"That" is the complementizer
that introduces

01:06:08.300 --> 01:06:10.185 align:middle line:90%
that embedded clause.

01:06:10.185 --> 01:06:11.810 align:middle line:84%
"I don't know whether
he ate the ants."

01:06:11.810 --> 01:06:15.050 align:middle line:84%
"Whether" is a complementizer
that can be selected by "know."

01:06:15.050 --> 01:06:17.840 align:middle line:84%
You can't "think whether
he ate the ants."

01:06:17.840 --> 01:06:20.270 align:middle line:84%
And actually there are
verbs like "know" that

01:06:20.270 --> 01:06:23.510 align:middle line:84%
can have either
"whether" or "that."

01:06:23.510 --> 01:06:25.700 align:middle line:84%
With a verb like "know"
that can have "whether,"

01:06:25.700 --> 01:06:29.670 align:middle line:84%
it can also have "what"
immediately following it.

01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:33.590 align:middle line:84%
So you can say, "I
don't know what he ate."

01:06:33.590 --> 01:06:36.320 align:middle line:84%
And notice that
if it has "what,"

01:06:36.320 --> 01:06:38.420 align:middle line:84%
it cannot have either
"that" or "whether."

01:06:38.420 --> 01:06:43.550 align:middle line:84%
So you can say, "I know
that he ate the ants."

01:06:43.550 --> 01:06:46.910 align:middle line:84%
You can say, "I know
whether he ate the ants."

01:06:46.910 --> 01:06:48.683 align:middle line:84%
And you can say, "I
know what he ate."

01:06:48.683 --> 01:06:50.600 align:middle line:84%
But you cannot say, "I
know what that he ate,"

01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:51.890 align:middle line:90%
or "what whether he ate."

01:06:51.890 --> 01:06:54.020 align:middle line:84%
So "that" and "whether"
and "what"-- they all

01:06:54.020 --> 01:06:55.940 align:middle line:84%
seem to be sort of in
the same slot there.

01:06:55.940 --> 01:06:58.945 align:middle line:90%
They're in the CP somehow.

01:06:58.945 --> 01:07:00.820 align:middle line:84%
We'll see they're not
quite in the same slot.

01:07:00.820 --> 01:07:02.710 align:middle line:84%
But the idea that
"what" ends up somewhere

01:07:02.710 --> 01:07:07.330 align:middle line:84%
in CP, that seems to be part
of what's going on here.

01:07:07.330 --> 01:07:10.040 align:middle line:90%


01:07:10.040 --> 01:07:13.010 align:middle line:84%
And then, as I said,
I promised you there

01:07:13.010 --> 01:07:16.190 align:middle line:84%
will be various reasons to
think that "what" starts off

01:07:16.190 --> 01:07:19.040 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "write"
and moves into CP.

01:07:19.040 --> 01:07:21.860 align:middle line:84%
But one of them is the
one I just went through.

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:24.680 align:middle line:84%
Under certain circumstances,
communicative circumstances,

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:26.845 align:middle line:84%
I can say, "Mary wrote
what on a typewriter?"

01:07:26.845 --> 01:07:28.220 align:middle line:84%
like if I couldn't
quite hear you

01:07:28.220 --> 01:07:31.610 align:middle line:84%
or if I don't believe
what you just said.

01:07:31.610 --> 01:07:33.830 align:middle line:84%
So here's a tree
that we're going

01:07:33.830 --> 01:07:36.860 align:middle line:84%
to draw for "What
will Mary write?"

01:07:36.860 --> 01:07:39.200 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have
"what" starting off

01:07:39.200 --> 01:07:41.780 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "write,"
right where the Projection

01:07:41.780 --> 01:07:43.250 align:middle line:90%
Principle wants it to be.

01:07:43.250 --> 01:07:46.310 align:middle line:84%
"Write" selects for a direct
object, and it gets one.

01:07:46.310 --> 01:07:47.840 align:middle line:90%
That's the sister of "write."

01:07:47.840 --> 01:07:52.790 align:middle line:84%
And then it moves to
the edge of the CP.

01:07:52.790 --> 01:07:56.480 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the kind of
tree that we're going to draw.

01:07:56.480 --> 01:07:59.400 align:middle line:84%
This phenomenon--
there's a name for it.

01:07:59.400 --> 01:08:01.950 align:middle line:90%
It's called wh-movement.

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:04.850 align:middle line:84%
It's called wh-movement
because these question words--

01:08:04.850 --> 01:08:10.640 align:middle line:84%
these words like "what" and
"where" and "why" and "when"--

01:08:10.640 --> 01:08:15.350 align:middle line:84%
tend to start with the
letters W and H in English.

01:08:15.350 --> 01:08:17.840 align:middle line:84%
"Who" and "what" and "where"
and "when" and "why"--

01:08:17.840 --> 01:08:22.430 align:middle line:84%
and "how," which doesn't start
with the letters W and H,

01:08:22.430 --> 01:08:24.560 align:middle line:84%
but it does contain
the letters W and H,

01:08:24.560 --> 01:08:26.130 align:middle line:90%
just not in the right order.

01:08:26.130 --> 01:08:28.939 align:middle line:84%
So these words are
called wh-words.

01:08:28.939 --> 01:08:30.050 align:middle line:90%
And they're called that.

01:08:30.050 --> 01:08:32.029 align:middle line:84%
It's just a technical
term in syntax.

01:08:32.029 --> 01:08:35.300 align:middle line:84%
We call these words wh-words,
regardless of the language

01:08:35.300 --> 01:08:36.229 align:middle line:90%
we're working in.

01:08:36.229 --> 01:08:39.367 align:middle line:84%
It's a sort of embarrassingly
Anglocentric term.

01:08:39.367 --> 01:08:41.450 align:middle line:84%
So they're called wh-words
because in English they

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:42.979 align:middle line:90%
start with W and H.

01:08:42.979 --> 01:08:44.479 align:middle line:84%
The language that
you're working on,

01:08:44.479 --> 01:08:46.370 align:middle line:84%
they surely will not
start with W and H.

01:08:46.370 --> 01:08:49.160 align:middle line:84%
But we're still going to
want to call them wh-words.

01:08:49.160 --> 01:08:52.399 align:middle line:84%
And this phenomenon
is called wh-movement.

01:08:52.399 --> 01:08:55.310 align:middle line:84%
And it's a cross-linguistically
very common phenomenon.

01:08:55.310 --> 01:08:57.904 align:middle line:90%


01:08:57.904 --> 01:08:59.779 align:middle line:84%
There's an English
example of it there again.

01:08:59.779 --> 01:09:01.208 align:middle line:90%
"What did you put on the table?"

01:09:01.208 --> 01:09:03.500 align:middle line:84%
Here's the Tagalog for "What
did you put on the table?"

01:09:03.500 --> 01:09:06.800 align:middle line:84%
You can see the Tagalog word
for "what" doesn't begin with W

01:09:06.800 --> 01:09:10.520 align:middle line:84%
and H. Tagalog
does have W and H,

01:09:10.520 --> 01:09:13.220 align:middle line:84%
but words can't
start with W and H.

01:09:13.220 --> 01:09:17.213 align:middle line:84%
And there's the Finnish for
"where did I put my clothes."

01:09:17.213 --> 01:09:19.130 align:middle line:84%
I should really learn
how to say "what did you

01:09:19.130 --> 01:09:20.660 align:middle line:90%
put on the table" in Finnish.

01:09:20.660 --> 01:09:23.069 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why that's
my Finnish example.

01:09:23.069 --> 01:09:26.750 align:middle line:84%
So various unrelated languages
that have wh-movement.

01:09:26.750 --> 01:09:30.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a cross-linguistically
very common phenomenon.

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:32.062 align:middle line:90%
It's not universal.

01:09:32.062 --> 01:09:33.770 align:middle line:84%
Some of you, in fact,
are native speakers

01:09:33.770 --> 01:09:37.640 align:middle line:84%
of languages in which
wh-words don't move

01:09:37.640 --> 01:09:39.859 align:middle line:90%
to the left edge of the clause.

01:09:39.859 --> 01:09:41.910 align:middle line:90%
They just stay where they are.

01:09:41.910 --> 01:09:44.314 align:middle line:84%
So in Chinese, for
example, if you

01:09:44.314 --> 01:09:45.814 align:middle line:84%
want to say "What
did Zhangsan buy,"

01:09:45.814 --> 01:09:48.120 align:middle line:84%
you don't say, "What
did Zhangsan buy,"

01:09:48.120 --> 01:09:49.370 align:middle line:90%
or at least you don't have to.

01:09:49.370 --> 01:09:51.260 align:middle line:84%
The standard thing
to say is, literally,

01:09:51.260 --> 01:09:53.930 align:middle line:84%
"Zhangsan bought what,"
which, as I said,

01:09:53.930 --> 01:09:57.230 align:middle line:84%
you can say in English, but only
under special circumstances.

01:09:57.230 --> 01:09:58.940 align:middle line:84%
In Chinese, that's
just the natural way

01:09:58.940 --> 01:10:01.520 align:middle line:84%
to ask that question, is
to leave [INAUDIBLE] right

01:10:01.520 --> 01:10:04.580 align:middle line:84%
where it would be as
the object of the verb.

01:10:04.580 --> 01:10:06.680 align:middle line:84%
And here are a couple
more examples--

01:10:06.680 --> 01:10:08.690 align:middle line:84%
Bafut, which is a
language of Cameroon,

01:10:08.690 --> 01:10:12.200 align:middle line:84%
and Hopi, which is a language
of the American Southwest.

01:10:12.200 --> 01:10:15.100 align:middle line:90%


01:10:15.100 --> 01:10:18.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's
cross-linguistic variation

01:10:18.160 --> 01:10:19.635 align:middle line:90%
in how you form wh-questions.

01:10:19.635 --> 01:10:21.010 align:middle line:84%
There are languages,
like English

01:10:21.010 --> 01:10:23.350 align:middle line:84%
and Finnish and Tagalog and
many other languages, where

01:10:23.350 --> 01:10:25.240 align:middle line:84%
you take your wh-words
and you move them

01:10:25.240 --> 01:10:27.600 align:middle line:90%
to the left edge of CP.

01:10:27.600 --> 01:10:29.350 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
languages, like Chinese

01:10:29.350 --> 01:10:32.600 align:middle line:84%
and Bafut and Hopi, where
the wh-words just don't move.

01:10:32.600 --> 01:10:36.060 align:middle line:84%
They just stay where
they would normally go,

01:10:36.060 --> 01:10:38.060 align:middle line:84%
wherever they would
normally go in the sentence.

01:10:38.060 --> 01:10:39.380 align:middle line:90%
There's no movement going on.

01:10:39.380 --> 01:10:42.820 align:middle line:84%
So in Chinese, if
you wanted to say,

01:10:42.820 --> 01:10:44.950 align:middle line:84%
"Zhangsan bought a
book," instead of "what,"

01:10:44.950 --> 01:10:48.160 align:middle line:84%
you would just put
the word for "book."

01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:50.270 align:middle line:84%
Chinese normal word
order is subject,

01:10:50.270 --> 01:10:51.940 align:middle line:84%
verb, object, kind
of like English.

01:10:51.940 --> 01:10:53.380 align:middle line:90%
Same deal for Bafut.

01:10:53.380 --> 01:10:55.270 align:middle line:84%
Hopi has the verb at
the end of the sentence.

01:10:55.270 --> 01:10:58.702 align:middle line:84%
So these wh-words, these
words that mean things

01:10:58.702 --> 01:11:00.160 align:middle line:84%
like "what" and
"who," they're just

01:11:00.160 --> 01:11:02.050 align:middle line:84%
going where the object
would normally go

01:11:02.050 --> 01:11:04.910 align:middle line:90%
in these wh-in-situ languages.

01:11:04.910 --> 01:11:08.660 align:middle line:84%
And those are almost
the only two options.

01:11:08.660 --> 01:11:11.690 align:middle line:84%
Take your wh-word and move
it to the left edge of CP,

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:14.720 align:middle line:84%
or leave it where it
would normally go.

01:11:14.720 --> 01:11:16.550 align:middle line:90%
You can imagine others, right?

01:11:16.550 --> 01:11:20.240 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, it's
not clear that there

01:11:20.240 --> 01:11:22.400 align:middle line:84%
are any languages in
which you take the wh-word

01:11:22.400 --> 01:11:24.945 align:middle line:84%
and you move it to
the end of the clause.

01:11:24.945 --> 01:11:26.570 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine a
language like that,

01:11:26.570 --> 01:11:27.890 align:middle line:84%
but it's not clear
that there are

01:11:27.890 --> 01:11:30.390 align:middle line:84%
any languages in which you say,
"You put on the table what?"

01:11:30.390 --> 01:11:33.110 align:middle line:90%


01:11:33.110 --> 01:11:37.400 align:middle line:84%
I think my next slides make that
observation more complicated.

01:11:37.400 --> 01:11:39.687 align:middle line:84%
And I think I want to
skip them, actually.

01:11:39.687 --> 01:11:41.270 align:middle line:84%
So to make this fact
more interesting,

01:11:41.270 --> 01:11:44.288 align:middle line:84%
let me show you one other kind
of cross-linguistic variation.

01:11:44.288 --> 01:11:45.330 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to do that.

01:11:45.330 --> 01:11:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So let me do that fast.

01:11:46.580 --> 01:11:49.940 align:middle line:84%
We'll come back to this because
we're running low on time.

01:11:49.940 --> 01:11:51.830 align:middle line:84%
And I want to show you
one other quick thing.

01:11:51.830 --> 01:11:56.053 align:middle line:90%


01:11:56.053 --> 01:11:57.470 align:middle line:84%
In the remaining
five minutes, I'm

01:11:57.470 --> 01:11:59.090 align:middle line:90%
going to set up another thing.

01:11:59.090 --> 01:12:03.230 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll come back, and
we'll start here next time.

01:12:03.230 --> 01:12:06.290 align:middle line:84%
I've been concentrating on
wh-questions where you're only

01:12:06.290 --> 01:12:08.150 align:middle line:90%
asking about one thing.

01:12:08.150 --> 01:12:10.410 align:middle line:84%
In many languages--
not all languages,

01:12:10.410 --> 01:12:12.260 align:middle line:84%
but in many languages,
it's possible to ask

01:12:12.260 --> 01:12:14.280 align:middle line:84%
about more than one
thing at the same time.

01:12:14.280 --> 01:12:16.943 align:middle line:84%
So you can ask questions like,
"What did you give to whom,"

01:12:16.943 --> 01:12:18.860 align:middle line:84%
where, if I ask you a
question like that, what

01:12:18.860 --> 01:12:23.870 align:middle line:84%
I want is a list of people
and things such that person

01:12:23.870 --> 01:12:28.052 align:middle line:84%
x got thing x and
person y got thing y.

01:12:28.052 --> 01:12:30.635 align:middle line:84%
That's what I'm asking you if I
ask you that kind of question.

01:12:30.635 --> 01:12:36.060 align:middle line:90%


01:12:36.060 --> 01:12:37.630 align:middle line:90%
Again, there's some variation.

01:12:37.630 --> 01:12:40.650 align:middle line:84%
So English, as I said, is a
language with wh-movement.

01:12:40.650 --> 01:12:44.340 align:middle line:84%
But it's specifically a language
with wh-movement of one WH.

01:12:44.340 --> 01:12:46.800 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to ask
a multiple wh-question,

01:12:46.800 --> 01:12:49.230 align:middle line:84%
you move one of your
wh-words, but you leave

01:12:49.230 --> 01:12:50.640 align:middle line:90%
the other ones where they are.

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:52.445 align:middle line:84%
So you say, "What did
you give to whom?"

01:12:52.445 --> 01:12:55.860 align:middle line:84%
or "What did you give to
whom when, where, why, how?"

01:12:55.860 --> 01:12:57.720 align:middle line:84%
So all your other
wh-words just stay

01:12:57.720 --> 01:12:59.880 align:middle line:90%
where they would normally go.

01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:03.120 align:middle line:84%
There are other languages
in which all of the wh-words

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:07.870 align:middle line:84%
move in this big, moving herd
to the beginning of the clause.

01:13:07.870 --> 01:13:10.140 align:middle line:84%
So in Bulgarian,
for example, you

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:12.570 align:middle line:84%
don't ask literally, "What
did you give to whom?"

01:13:12.570 --> 01:13:16.390 align:middle line:84%
You ask literally, "What
to whom did he give?"

01:13:16.390 --> 01:13:18.900 align:middle line:84%
So "what" and "to whom"
are both at the beginning

01:13:18.900 --> 01:13:19.710 align:middle line:90%
of the sentence.

01:13:19.710 --> 01:13:21.335 align:middle line:84%
There are bunch of
languages like this.

01:13:21.335 --> 01:13:23.070 align:middle line:90%
Mohawk is another one.

01:13:23.070 --> 01:13:27.390 align:middle line:84%
Mohawk's a language spoken
not too far from here,

01:13:27.390 --> 01:13:30.620 align:middle line:90%
in parts of America and Canada.

01:13:30.620 --> 01:13:33.400 align:middle line:84%
These are both languages
in which the wh-words all

01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:35.290 align:middle line:84%
have to move in a
multiple wh-question.

01:13:35.290 --> 01:13:38.930 align:middle line:90%


01:13:38.930 --> 01:13:42.350 align:middle line:84%
And again, there's some
variation between languages.

01:13:42.350 --> 01:13:47.690 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that move
one wh-word, like English.

01:13:47.690 --> 01:13:50.330 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that move
no wh-words, like Chinese.

01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:52.460 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
move all the wh-words,

01:13:52.460 --> 01:13:54.980 align:middle line:90%
like Bulgarian or Mohawk.

01:13:54.980 --> 01:13:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And again, it's not too hard
to imagine other options.

01:13:57.660 --> 01:13:59.870 align:middle line:84%
So for example, you
could imagine a language

01:13:59.870 --> 01:14:02.810 align:middle line:90%
that only moves two wh-words.

01:14:02.810 --> 01:14:04.460 align:middle line:84%
That would be a
language where you

01:14:04.460 --> 01:14:07.940 align:middle line:84%
would say, "Who
what gave to whom?"

01:14:07.940 --> 01:14:11.750 align:middle line:90%
to mean who gave what to whom.

01:14:11.750 --> 01:14:13.358 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any
languages like that.

01:14:13.358 --> 01:14:14.900 align:middle line:84%
The only kinds of
languages there are

01:14:14.900 --> 01:14:16.700 align:middle line:90%
are the English kind--

01:14:16.700 --> 01:14:18.620 align:middle line:84%
so first, the Mandarin
kind, the Chinese kind,

01:14:18.620 --> 01:14:19.790 align:middle line:84%
where you don't
move any of them;

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:22.310 align:middle line:84%
the English kind, where you
move one, where you say, "Who

01:14:22.310 --> 01:14:25.370 align:middle line:84%
gave what to whom?"; and the
Bulgarian/Mohawk kind, where

01:14:25.370 --> 01:14:26.120 align:middle line:90%
you move them all.

01:14:26.120 --> 01:14:28.460 align:middle line:84%
You say, "Who what
to whom gave?"

01:14:28.460 --> 01:14:31.130 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any languages
like this that just

01:14:31.130 --> 01:14:33.390 align:middle line:90%
move two or move up to two.

01:14:33.390 --> 01:14:37.380 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine a language like
that, but there aren't any.

01:14:37.380 --> 01:14:39.960 align:middle line:84%
And let me just leave
you with this last point,

01:14:39.960 --> 01:14:42.465 align:middle line:84%
and we'll probably take it
up here again next time.

01:14:42.465 --> 01:14:46.020 align:middle line:90%


01:14:46.020 --> 01:14:49.470 align:middle line:84%
Logical problem of
language acquisition--

01:14:49.470 --> 01:14:50.820 align:middle line:90%
here's a game we can play.

01:14:50.820 --> 01:14:52.060 align:middle line:90%
Here's a function.

01:14:52.060 --> 01:14:54.450 align:middle line:84%
It's a function that if you
give it 1, the answer is 1.

01:14:54.450 --> 01:14:55.530 align:middle line:84%
If you give it 2,
the answer is 2.

01:14:55.530 --> 01:14:56.947 align:middle line:84%
If you give it 3,
the answer is 3.

01:14:56.947 --> 01:14:58.410 align:middle line:84%
If you give it 4,
the answer is 4.

01:14:58.410 --> 01:15:01.020 align:middle line:84%
What do you think the
answer is if you give it 5?

01:15:01.020 --> 01:15:01.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 5.

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:02.990 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: 5?

01:15:02.990 --> 01:15:04.830 align:middle line:84%
Can anybody think of
any other options?

01:15:04.830 --> 01:15:05.330 align:middle line:90%
Ha!

01:15:05.330 --> 01:15:06.740 align:middle line:90%
You've fallen into my trap.

01:15:06.740 --> 01:15:09.957 align:middle line:84%
The function that I actually
had in mind was this one.

01:15:09.957 --> 01:15:11.420 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:15:11.420 --> 01:15:12.800 align:middle line:90%
Such that the answer is 29.

01:15:12.800 --> 01:15:16.190 align:middle line:84%
In fact, the answer
could be anything at all.

01:15:16.190 --> 01:15:18.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is the kind of
question you sometimes get

01:15:18.830 --> 01:15:20.300 align:middle line:90%
asked on standardized tests.

01:15:20.300 --> 01:15:23.820 align:middle line:84%
And it's always unfair because
the answer could be anything

01:15:23.820 --> 01:15:24.320 align:middle line:90%
at all.

01:15:24.320 --> 01:15:26.150 align:middle line:84%
That first thing
which I carefully

01:15:26.150 --> 01:15:29.360 align:middle line:84%
devised to be 0 as long as the
thing I was giving the function

01:15:29.360 --> 01:15:32.880 align:middle line:84%
was 1, 2, 3, or 4, you could
multiply that by anything.

01:15:32.880 --> 01:15:36.590 align:middle line:90%
And so 5 could be any number.

01:15:36.590 --> 01:15:37.855 align:middle line:90%
Life is like that a lot.

01:15:37.855 --> 01:15:39.980 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of places
where you go through life,

01:15:39.980 --> 01:15:42.710 align:middle line:84%
and you get a certain number
of finite observations

01:15:42.710 --> 01:15:44.630 align:middle line:90%
of how life works.

01:15:44.630 --> 01:15:46.160 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
to make a decision

01:15:46.160 --> 01:15:47.690 align:middle line:90%
about what the basic rule is.

01:15:47.690 --> 01:15:49.335 align:middle line:84%
So you're doing
science, let's say.

01:15:49.335 --> 01:15:50.210 align:middle line:90%
And you go out there.

01:15:50.210 --> 01:15:54.253 align:middle line:90%
You see many white swans.

01:15:54.253 --> 01:15:55.670 align:middle line:84%
At some point, you
have to decide,

01:15:55.670 --> 01:15:57.500 align:middle line:84%
have I seen enough
white swans that I get

01:15:57.500 --> 01:15:59.360 align:middle line:90%
to decide that swans are white?

01:15:59.360 --> 01:16:01.250 align:middle line:84%
Have I seen enough
black crows that I get

01:16:01.250 --> 01:16:03.260 align:middle line:90%
to say that crows are black?

01:16:03.260 --> 01:16:04.040 align:middle line:90%
You never know.

01:16:04.040 --> 01:16:06.940 align:middle line:84%
Maybe the next one is going
to be purple or green.

01:16:06.940 --> 01:16:08.090 align:middle line:90%
It's just hard to know.

01:16:08.090 --> 01:16:12.470 align:middle line:84%
And this function is sort of
a dramatic example of that.

01:16:12.470 --> 01:16:15.080 align:middle line:84%
Imagine, just in the
remaining seconds,

01:16:15.080 --> 01:16:17.930 align:middle line:90%
that you are a Bulgarian child.

01:16:17.930 --> 01:16:20.550 align:middle line:84%
Were any of you
Bulgarian children?

01:16:20.550 --> 01:16:22.520 align:middle line:84%
OK, so just imagine
counterfactually

01:16:22.520 --> 01:16:24.950 align:middle line:90%
that you were a Bulgarian child.

01:16:24.950 --> 01:16:27.560 align:middle line:84%
Here you are,
growing up, hearing

01:16:27.560 --> 01:16:30.620 align:middle line:90%
your parents ask wh-questions.

01:16:30.620 --> 01:16:34.520 align:middle line:84%
And there's got to
be some number, n,

01:16:34.520 --> 01:16:37.790 align:middle line:84%
that is the largest
number of wh-words

01:16:37.790 --> 01:16:40.370 align:middle line:90%
you ever heard in a question.

01:16:40.370 --> 01:16:44.660 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you heard your parents
say two wh-words in a question.

01:16:44.660 --> 01:16:46.520 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you heard them say three.

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:48.110 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of unlikely
that you ever

01:16:48.110 --> 01:16:50.240 align:middle line:90%
heard them say four or five.

01:16:50.240 --> 01:16:53.030 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it depends, I guess, on
what your parents' line of work

01:16:53.030 --> 01:16:54.080 align:middle line:90%
was.

01:16:54.080 --> 01:16:56.270 align:middle line:84%
But there's some number
that's the largest number.

01:16:56.270 --> 01:16:59.360 align:middle line:84%
And it's probably different
from Bulgarian to Bulgarian.

01:16:59.360 --> 01:17:00.780 align:middle line:90%
So what we might expect--

01:17:00.780 --> 01:17:03.140 align:middle line:84%
so Bulgarians are sort
of in the position

01:17:03.140 --> 01:17:06.290 align:middle line:84%
you guys were in
with that function.

01:17:06.290 --> 01:17:09.260 align:middle line:84%
If you just hear this,
let's say, "What to whom

01:17:09.260 --> 01:17:10.910 align:middle line:90%
did he give?"

01:17:10.910 --> 01:17:13.130 align:middle line:84%
you've got to ask
yourself, is the rule move

01:17:13.130 --> 01:17:14.540 align:middle line:90%
all the wh-phrases?

01:17:14.540 --> 01:17:16.670 align:middle line:84%
Is it move two of
the wh-phrases?

01:17:16.670 --> 01:17:20.300 align:middle line:84%
Is it move maximally three,
maximally four, whatever?

01:17:20.300 --> 01:17:23.120 align:middle line:84%
There are literally
infinitely many rules

01:17:23.120 --> 01:17:26.370 align:middle line:84%
that are compatible
with this data point.

01:17:26.370 --> 01:17:28.800 align:middle line:84%
And so you might have expected
that Bulgarians, depending

01:17:28.800 --> 01:17:31.320 align:middle line:84%
on how weird their parents
were, would grow up

01:17:31.320 --> 01:17:32.700 align:middle line:84%
with slightly
different grammars,

01:17:32.700 --> 01:17:34.860 align:middle line:84%
that when you took
Bulgarians and you put them

01:17:34.860 --> 01:17:39.600 align:middle line:84%
into a hyperbaric chamber and
you gradually added wh-words

01:17:39.600 --> 01:17:41.640 align:middle line:84%
to the questions, that
they would diverge

01:17:41.640 --> 01:17:43.650 align:middle line:84%
as the questions got more
and more complicated,

01:17:43.650 --> 01:17:46.500 align:middle line:84%
that there would be
the two-wh Bulgarians

01:17:46.500 --> 01:17:49.305 align:middle line:84%
and the three-wh Bulgarians
and the four and the five,

01:17:49.305 --> 01:17:50.890 align:middle line:90%
that you would find that out.

01:17:50.890 --> 01:17:52.290 align:middle line:90%
That's not what we find out.

01:17:52.290 --> 01:17:54.330 align:middle line:90%
The experiment has been done.

01:17:54.330 --> 01:17:56.740 align:middle line:84%
What you find out is the
Bulgarians are all the same.

01:17:56.740 --> 01:18:00.420 align:middle line:84%
They all move all
their wh-phrases.

01:18:00.420 --> 01:18:04.890 align:middle line:84%
What we think is going on really
is that being a human being

01:18:04.890 --> 01:18:08.040 align:middle line:84%
means having the kind of mind
that can put language together

01:18:08.040 --> 01:18:10.960 align:middle line:90%
in some ways but not others.

01:18:10.960 --> 01:18:14.040 align:middle line:84%
There was never any danger
of the Bulgarian children

01:18:14.040 --> 01:18:16.140 align:middle line:84%
entertaining the
possibility that the grammar

01:18:16.140 --> 01:18:18.240 align:middle line:90%
was move two wh-phrases.

01:18:18.240 --> 01:18:21.030 align:middle line:84%
They knew that there
aren't languages like that.

01:18:21.030 --> 01:18:23.050 align:middle line:84%
Human languages
don't work that way.

01:18:23.050 --> 01:18:25.980 align:middle line:84%
This is what we mean when we
talk about universal grammar.

01:18:25.980 --> 01:18:28.350 align:middle line:84%
We'll review this next time
because we're out of time.

01:18:28.350 --> 01:18:31.220 align:middle line:84%
But we'll talk more
about this next time.

01:18:31.220 --> 01:18:37.000 align:middle line:90%